\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Indonesia's c.bank holds key rate amid 'escalating' global uncertainty\b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2016\par 12:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * BI keeps rate at 4.75 pct, as widely expected\par \par \uc2 * Economic growth forecast for 2017 revised down\par \par \uc2 * C.bank appears more worried about rupiah than growth - economist\par \par \uc2 * Governor: We'll maintain system of free flow of capital (Adds governor saying Indonesia won't impose capital controls)\par \par \uc2 By Nilufar Rizki and Gayatri Suroyo\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Nov 17 (Reuters) - \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing "escalating" global economic uncertainty, on Thursday held its key interest rate steady as expected and trimmed its growth forecast for 2017.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia (BI) \uc2 maintained its benchmark interest rate at 4.75 percent, as forecast by 18 out of 19 analysts in a Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 Analysts had said market volatility following \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's victory meant BI could not cut its benchmark for a seventh time this year.\par \par \uc2 The rate hold "is in line with BI's cautiousness in responding to the escalating uncertainty in the global financial market after the U.S. election," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the U.S. election, foreign investors sold around 19 trillion rupiah ($523.4 million) of Indonesian stocks and bonds, according to data from the stock exchange and the Finance Ministry.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah fell sharply after the vote, but then stabilised with the help of BI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure it did not drop too rapidly. On Thursday, it was trading at around 13,375 to the dollar, or 2 percent weaker than before Trump's win.\par \par \uc2 BI Governor Agus Martowardojo told reporters on Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in line with market mechanisms. He affirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not impose capital controls.\par \par \uc2 "We would like to emphasize that Indonesia will maintain the system of free flow of capital," he said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts says the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come under pressure again if the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raises interest rates in December.\par \par \uc2 BI thinks the Fed will hike the U.S. rates by 25 basis points next month, its deputy governor said, and then five more times until the end of 2018.\par \par \uc2 END OF A CYCLE?\par \par \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  said Thursday's hold "suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently more concerned with maintaining a stable exchange rate than supporting the economy. With the U.S. set to start hiking interest rates soon, we think BI's easing cycle is nearing an end."\par \par \uc2 Weiwen Ng of ANZ said he expects BI "to maintain a neutral monetary policy stance and remain on hold for an extended period of time."\par \par \uc2 BI has cut its main policy rate this year by a total of 150 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Despite the cuts, loan growth has continued to weaken. As of September, annual expansion of outstanding loans was at its weakest in nearly seven years, 6.47 percent, as commercial banks grappled with increased levels of bad loans.\par \par \uc2 Sluggish loan expansion was one reason annual growth in Southeast Asia's largest economy decelerated to 5.02 percent in the third quarter, from 5.18 percent in April-June.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, BI amended its forecast for 2016 growth to 5.0 percent instead of a range of 4.9-5.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The forecast for 2017 was changed to 5.0-5.4 percent, rather than 5.1-5.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 "BI faces a difficult balancing act," \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  said. "Despite having cut interest rates six times this year already, the domestic economy could clearly do with some additional support... But the threat of further falls in the rupiah means that BI is likely to act with caution." ($1 = 13,375 rupiah)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Hidayat Setiaji, Eveline Danubrata and Fransiska Nangoy; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-17T12:18:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161117ecbh00fwp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
