\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Fall as Trump Influences Investors\b0\par\par\uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2016\par 04:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds continued their post-U.S.-election selloff on Monday despite more positive economic data from the region, as investors piled on bets that U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's economic plans could cause the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates more aggressively next year.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen dropped to its weakest level in five months against the dollar even though Japan's economy grew at a better-than-expected rate of 2.2% in the third quarter. The yen was last down 0.8% at 107.49, continuing its slide since the U.S. election outcome sparked a broad-based rise in the dollar against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah, Korean won and Indian rupee were among several emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to suffer against the U.S. dollar on Monday, while \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan at a more than seven-year low versus the greenback in mainland markets.\par \par \uc2 There was a sharp move upward, meanwhile, in the yield on 10-year government bonds in Malaysia and Indonesia, while the yield on similar bonds in Japan reached -0.012, its highest level since Sept. 21 when the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  announced a new policy to pin yields near zero.\par \par \uc2 The dramatic recent move in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds stems largely from a concurrent surge in yields on U.S. Treasurys since the surprise presidential election result: on Monday, the yield on 30-year Treasurys rose above 3% in Asian trading hours for the first time since January. Investors are reasoning that Mr. Trump's plans to spend more on areas such as infrastructure could spark higher inflation, leading the Fed to raise interest rates sooner than expected.\par \par \uc2 As U.S. bonds have become more attractive, money that had flowed into emerging markets this year has gone into reverse, pushing up the dollar's value and raising yields on Asian government debt. Bond yields rise as their prices fall.\par \par \uc2 For now, rising U.S. bond yields are outweighing investors' positive thoughts about Asian economies. Beside Monday's strong GDP numbers from Japan, Malaysia last week reported better-than-expected third-quarter growth, rising to 4.3% from 4% in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 The selloff "appears to have been driven by a general rise in investor risk aversion, rather than specific concerns about U.S. policy shifts, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar's strength against a basket of six \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rose 0.5% to 99.591, its highest level since January, as yields on the U.S. 10-year Treasury note rose to 2.19%.\par \par \uc2 "Surging inflation expectations arising from U.S. president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's pro-growth stance have raised risks of capital outflows from the [Asian] region including China," wrote Qi Gao, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for emerging Asia at Scotiabank.\par \par \uc2 The current market trend echoes the summer of 2013, when signs that the Fed was about to end quantitative-easing program caused investors to withdraw from emerging markets\u8212\'20\'14an episode dubbed the "taper tantrum."\par \par \uc2 Some $38 billion flowed out from emerging-market stocks and bonds between June and August 2013, according to analysts at \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . Since the U.S. election some $3.3 billion has flowed out of Asian equities alone, the analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have kept sliding despite signs of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from some central banks. The dollar rose by 1.1% against the Malaysian ringgit despite a warning from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  there on Sunday against speculative ringgit trading in offshore markets.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah meanwhile resumed its selloff Monday, following an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday that had strengthened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The rupiah weakened by as much as 0.9% against the U.S. dollar before last trading 0.5% weaker at 13,355.00.\par \par \uc2 Selling in Asia's government bonds also gathered pace on Monday, as rising \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields threaten to reverse a trend that has attracted yield-starved investors to emerging markets for much of the year. The risk of capital flight is most acute for countries like Malaysia and Indonesia, where foreign ownership of government debt is particularly high.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Malaysia's 10-year government bond rose to its highest level since January, last at 4.080% compared with 3.820% late Friday. The yield on a similar bond in Indonesia was last at 7.892%, compared with 7.469% late Friday.\par \par \uc2 If Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "continue to weaken, that triggers inflation, and yields will rise even further," said Frances Cheung, head of rates strategy, Asia ex-Japan, at Soci\u233\'00\'E9 t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9 n\u233\'00\'E9 rale in Hong Kong. Any perceptions of high capital outflows will trigger even more bond selling, pushing yields even higher, she added.\par \par \uc2 Kenan Machado contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gregor Stuart Hunter at gregor.hunter@wsj.com and Rachel Rosenthal at Rachel.Rosenthal@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 13, 2016 22:05 ET (03:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161114ecbe00024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
