\par\par \b \uc2 The Emerging Market Trade Goes Cold -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 899 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2016\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Rachel Rosenthal, Mike Bird and Julie Wernau \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are dumping assets in emerging markets around the world, as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 's election victory upends a long-profitable trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 A fresh selloff rolled from Asia into Europe, Africa and Latin America as the day progressed Friday. Stocks ended the day down 4% in Indonesia, while South Africa's FTSE \uc2 JSE\uc2  index fell by 2.3%. Argentina's Merval index tumbled 3.5%.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  took a beating, too. Even the Russian ruble, which held up in the election's immediate aftermath, dropped by 0.6% against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Investors are expected to pour a net $157 billion into emerging markets by the end of the year, according to the Institute for International Finance, seeking relief from the rock-bottom yields prevailing elsewhere around the world. But Mr. Trump's election has changed that calculus. His emphasis on infrastructure spending and tax cuts has sparked a rally in U.S. stocks and sent benchmark Treasury yields sharply higher.\par \par \uc2 With better yields now available in developed markets and expectations that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could have to raise key interest rates more aggressively, rather than the slow and gradual approach many analysts had been expecting, investors have a more compelling case to keep their money in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Emerging market stocks and bonds suffered about $2.4 billion in outflows over the past week, with much of that cash exiting since the election, the IIF said.\par \par \uc2 "Higher rates in the U.S. will naturally raise the bar for capital flows to emerging markets," said Mirza Baig, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest-rate strategy at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Yields on benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasurys surged above 2% this week for the first time since the beginning of the year. U.S. bond markets were closed Friday for Veterans Day. The shift could be a long-lasting one.\par \par \uc2 "The previous shallow hiking cycle is probably not going to happen," said Mark Baker, an emerging-markets portfolio manager at Standard Life Investments in Hong Kong. "I think there's a narrative around tighter global liquidity conditions that could become more important once again."\par \par \uc2 In that kind of environment, investors could rethink decisions to put money into countries that are reliant on foreign capital, such as Turkey and South Africa, Mr. Baker said.\par \par \uc2 The South African rand fell by more than 1% against the greenback on Friday. The yield on the country's sovereign bond maturing in 2026 rose to 9%, its highest since early September.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investment can be a critical source of capital for developing countries' infrastructure, construction and bank lending.\par \par \uc2 Countries including Mexico, Colombia and Brazil are just beginning to recover from expansionary fiscal policies that widened deficits, drove up inflation, and lowered growth. Many of these governments are suffering from low approval ratings as they enact painful budget cuts. Capital outflows could tip an already difficult balance in the wrong direction, said Daniel Kerner, Latin America practice head at \uc2 Eurasia Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "People pulling money out will hit many emerging markets, particularly in Latin America, at a very weak moment," Mr. Kerner said.\par \par \uc2 In Argentina, a new administration has repaired relations with foreign investors after a record breaking default in 2001. With billions of dollars flowing in, the country is working to repair everything from roads to energy infrastructure that had been neglected during its years as a pariah.\par \par \uc2 Foreign capital "tends to push down market interest rates, it means banks can borrow and lend and the net effect is to stimulate activity and growth," said Neil Shearing, chief emerging markets economist at Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  emerging-markets bond exchange-traded fund experienced its sharpest outflow on record on Thursday, with $300 million exiting the fund.\par \par \uc2 Latin America has been particularly hard-hit, given the region's close trading and immigration links with the U.S. A Brazilian government bond maturing in 2026 reached a yield of 5.25%, its highest since June. The Brazilian real's 5.3% decline against the dollar Thursday was the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest daily drop in five years. The Mexican peso is down more than 12% since the election.\par \par \uc2 A 2.7% slump in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rupiah prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asian companies had rushed to issue bonds in dollars before an expected U.S. rate increase in December. As the U.S. dollar strengthens against their home \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that debt will now likely become more burdensome to repay.\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets are sturdier than they were during the so-called taper tantrum of 2013, when worries that the Fed would pull back from its bond-buying program triggered heavy outflows. Current-account deficits in India and Indonesia have narrowed, for example.\par \par \uc2 Still, investors remain cautious given the uncertain outlook for the direction of Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 "If Treasury yields continue to march higher, eventually that will put pressure on emerging markets," said Ashley Perrott, head of pan-Asia fixed income at \uc2 UBS Asset Management\uc2  in Singapore. "What you think is an opportunity initially might turn into regret."\par \par \uc2 Write to Rachel Rosenthal at Rachel.Rosenthal@wsj.com, Mike Bird at Mike.Bird@wsj.com and Julie Wernau at Julie.Wernau@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 12, 2016 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161112ecbc0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
