\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Malaysia, Indonesia markets roiled as investors scramble for hedge on Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2016\par 10:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Malaysia c.bank comments in 11th para)\par \par \uc2 By Fransiska Nangoy and Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Emerging markets in Southeast Asia were slammed on Friday as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's shock victory in the U.S. presidential election reverberated around the world, with Malaysian and Indonesian central banks acting to try to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors sold stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 The latest selloff was triggered by investors revising their expectations, with a growing consensus that Trump's economic policies will be inflationary and push U.S. rates up faster than previously thought, driving funds out of emerging markets and into dollar-based assets.\par \par \uc2 Yields on benchmark 10-year Treasuries have spiked 41 basis points in the past two days as investors scrambled to readjust positions.\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets in Asia are particularly vulnerable to hot money outflows, and investors have been unsettled by the deep uncertainty over how broad U.S. and international policy will ultimately play out under Trump.\par \par \uc2 During Asian trading, the Malaysian ringgit sank to more than 12-year lows in the offshore market.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit's one-month non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) lost as much as 3.7 percent in the offshore market from the previous close to 4.5395 per dollar, its weakest since at least September 2004, according to \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the ringgit spot barely moved at 4.27 per dollar with liquidity extremely thin. As a result, the dollar/ringgit's NDFs premium over the dollar/ringgit spot increased to 0.2695, the widest since at least April 2008, according to \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2 , however, made sure the spot rate remained relatively stable in the onshore market, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Muhammad Ibrahim\uc2  told a news conference, called to release upbeat economic growth and current account data, that the ringgit should not be priced out of sync with fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 And, he said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a responsibility to tell banks to take temporary measures to calm the market.\par \par \uc2 "We don't want to be dictated by factors that have nothing to do with the country's fundamentals," Ibrahim said.\par \par \uc2 Later, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued a statement in which it added that it was providing necessary liquidity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Kuala Lumpur said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had told them not to quote offshore rates and was approving large ringgit sell orders on a case-by-case basis. The tactic seemed to work with onshore trade reportedly very thin.\par \par \uc2 HOT MONEY HEADACHE\par \par \uc2 However, rising yields on Malaysian government bonds showed how much pressure the ringgit has come under. Yields on 10-years have jumped 22 basis points since Wednesday, while those on 20-year and 30 year bonds have soared 21 basis points and 10 basis points, respectively, over the same period.\par \par \uc2 Almost 40 percent of Malaysian government bonds are in foreign hands.\par \par \uc2 Malaysian stocks were down almost one percent.\par \par \uc2 Indonesian markets also dived in early trade. Indonesia has enjoyed relatively high inflows into stocks and bonds markets in the past few months, making it vulnerable to hot money outflows at times of uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah fell as much as 2.6 percent, while the Jakarta Composite Index closed 4 percent lower, posting its biggest one-day drop since Aug 2013.\par \par \uc2 Nanang Hendarsah, an official at BI, said the rupiah's sharp drop was caused by sudden hedging activity in the NDF market, but noted outflows from Indonesian markets were contained so far.\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in both \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds markets, deputy governor Mirza Adityaswara said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to buy government bonds and will maintain the rupiah's value to reflect Indonesia's economic fundamentals, he said.\par \par \uc2 By trading drew to a close in Jakarta (0900 GMT), the rupiah had recouped some of its losses to stand 1.1 percent weaker than the previous close against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yield of Indonesia's 10-year government bonds jumped on Friday to 7.462 percent, its highest since July 1, from 7.417 percent. Foreigners own 38.4 percent of outstanding Indonesian government bonds.\par \par \uc2 Philippine stocks were also caught in the selloff with the main index tumbling more than 2.5 percent. The Philippines peso fell to 48.955, its weakest since September 2009.\par \par \uc2 For investors, particularly for those in emerging markets, the short term might prove a head-spinning affair.\par \par \uc2 "Mr Trump's economic policies present an upside risk to rates," said Khoon Goh, head of Asia research at ANZ.\par \par \uc2 "This, coupled with the depreciation pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has put serious pressure on Asia's carry trades."\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 13,490 rupiah) (Reporting by Fransiska Nangoy and Jongwoo Cheon; Additional reporting Hidayat Setiaji; Writing by Nachum Kaplan; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-14T