\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Trump effect hits Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , bonds; central banks rush to the rescue\b0\par\par\uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2016\par 07:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit 1-mth NDFs hits lowest in at least 12 years\par     * Rupiah at over 4-mth low; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  c.bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Philippine peso 7-year low; won touches 4-month trough\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par bonds plunged on Friday to extend weekly losses with growing\par fears of capital outflows in the expectation of higher U.S.\par interest rates under Donald Trump's presidency, prompting\par regional central banks to step in.\par      Malaysia's ringgit hit its weakest in more than 12 years in\par offshore markets as foreign investors dumped government bonds.\par Indonesia's rupiah slumped to its weakest in more than\par four months on bond outflows.\par     The Philippine peso touched a seven-year trough\par on equity outflows, while the South Korean won \par skidded to a more than four-month low as foreigners sold Seoul\par shares.\par     Singapore's dollar slumped to its weakest since\par Feb. 4 before turning firmer with talk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . China's yuan hit a six-year low. \par     "Our bearish view on Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is premised on Mr.\par Trump's pro-U.S. growth and pro-fiscal stimulus economic\par policies which will pose upside risks to U.S. rates, coupled\par with a protectionist stance on global trade," ANZ's analysts\par said in a note.\par     "The large portfolio flows into the region this year are at\par risk of a reversal."\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields jumped as Trump's policies are expected\par to lift inflation.\par     That hurt the appeal of higher yields in emerging Asia,\par compounding fears that his protectionist approach to trade will\par hit regional exports.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     Earlier,  ringgit one-month non-deliverable forwards\par  lost 3.7 percent from the previous close to 4.5395\par per dollar, the weakest since at least September 2004, according\par to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomson Reuters\f28 \uc2 \f2  data. Malaysia's 10-year government bond\par yield rose to 3.821 percent, the highest since June\par 28, as bond prices fell.\par     By contrast, spot ringgit stood at the previous\par close of 4.2700 per dollar in extremely thin liquidity.  \par     "We don't want to be dictated to by factors that have\par nothing to do with the country's fundamentals," \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Muhammad Ibrahim\f28 \uc2 \f2  told reporters. \par     He said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  had a responsibility to tell\par banks to take temporary measures to calm the market.\par  \par     Indonesian's rupiah was another victim of bond outflows. The\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell to 13,490 per dollar, its weakest since June 27,\par before recovering with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped in both the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and bond markets on\par Friday, deputy governor Mirza Adityaswara told reporters, adding\par that authorities stood ready to curb further volatility.\par  \par     Despite such efforts, the outlook for emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stays bearish, analysts said.\par     "High yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have to re-price to become\par attractive again," said Vaninder Singh, Asia economist at RBS in\par a note.\par     "On this count, the adjustment in Indonesia and Malaysia may\par have further to run: not just in matching the increase in U.S.\par yields, but also with higher spreads."\par     Spot ringgit has lost 1.7 percent against the dollar so far\par this week, while the rupiah has dropped 1.4 percent.\par     All other emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were set to suffer\par weekly losses.\par     The yuan has fallen near 0.9 percent throughout this week,\par which would be the largest weekly loss since early January.\par     The renminbi's weakness put further pressure on the\par Singapore dollar, which has lost almost 1.9 percent so far this\par week. The won has slipped 1.6 percent.\par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0530 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                 Latest bid   Previous day  Pct Move\par   Japan yen                   106.63         106.79     +0.15\par   Sing dlr                    1.4091         1.4117     +0.18\par   Taiwan dlr                  31.751         31.589     -0.51\par   Korean won                 1162.03        1150.60     -0.98\par   Baht                         35.26          35.11     -0.43\par   Peso                        48.920         48.660     -0.53\par   Rupiah                       13250          13135     -0.87\par   Rupee                        67.00          66.63     -0.56\par   Ringgit                     4.2