\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia shares, emerging  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  stumble as Trump anxiety deepens\b0\par\par\uc2 1,057 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2016\par 07:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Emerging markets hardest hit as U.S. bond yields soar\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  hardest hit, cbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * U.S. bond yields post biggest weekly rise since 2009\par \par \uc2 * Dollar gains on rising U.S. yields, hits high since July\par \par \uc2 * European shares seen opening 0.1-0.4 pct higher\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 11 (Reuters) - A searing selloff rocked Asian shares and emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday as investors feared higher U.S. interest rates under incoming President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will spark capital outflows from the region.\par \par \uc2 European shares are expected to open slightly higher after moderate losses in the previous session, with spread-betters seeing major European indexes, such as Britain's FTSE and Germany's DAX, rising 0.1 to 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell 1.4 percent as U.S. bond yields continued to soar on views that Trump's spending plans will push up inflation, possibly triggering more aggressive rate hikes by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets bore the brunt of selling, with MSCI emerging market index falling 1.5 percent to its lowest level since July, with Indonesia and Malaysia hit the hardest in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Indonesian shares slumped 3 percent while the rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen more than 2.5 percent to 4-1/2-month lows before it stabilised on the \uc2 Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit also dropped 1 percent to 9 1/2-month lows.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei bucked the trend, rising more than 1 percent to 6-1/2-month highs the yen weakened against the firming dollar.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the U.S. S&P 500 Index rose 0.2 percent while the \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  industrial average jumped 1.2 percent, smashing through its previous record high set in August by almost 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, the technology-heavy \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  fell 0.8 percent , with Apple dropping 2.8 percent, hit by fears that Trump's immigration policy could prevent Silicon Valley from attracting talent from around the world as it does now.\par \par \uc2 "The market's focus has shifted to Trump's policy after the initial knee-jerk risk-off reaction. The markets think he is likely to protect the U.S. domestic economy, especially the old economy," said Koichi Yoshikawa, executive director of financial markets at Standard Chartered Bank.\par \par \uc2 "That explains why the Dow was up and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  was weak," he added.\par \par \uc2 The financial sector surged 3.7 percent to its highest since the 2008 global financial crisis, as Trump has sided with leading conservatives in calling for the repeal of the 2010 Dodd-Frank Financial Reform Act largely opposed by banks.\par \par \uc2 U.S. bond markets have also seen dramatic moves since Trump's victory, with the 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield hitting their highest levels in 10 months.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that his policy stance - from protectionism and fiscal expansion - will boost inflation have been driving the surge in U.S. yields.\par \par \uc2 "You have fiscal policy that will widen the deficit by trillions of dollars in a decade, and if there's a 20 percent tariff on imports from China, that alone would boost inflation by one percentage point," said Tomoaki Shishido, fixed income analyst at \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The 10-year U.S. yield rose to 2.15 percent, almost 30 basis points, or 0.30 percentage point, above its levels around 1.86 percent just before the U.S. election on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Inflation expectations measured by U.S. inflation-linked bonds rose to 1.87 percent, its highest since July last year, up from low below 1.2 percent touched in February.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year yield rose 38 basis points, posting its biggest weekly jump since 2009 before a U.S. market holiday on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Soaring U.S. yields have been a boon to dollar bulls. The euro dipped to $1.0908, compared to $1.1025 before the U.S. elections.\par \par \uc2 The dollar strengthened sharply against the yen, which has traditionally a strong inverse correlation with U.S. yields because higher U.S. yields encourage Japanese investors to buy more U.S. debt.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to as high as 106.95 yen, its highest since late July, compared to around 105.15 yen before the elections. It eased back to 106.38 yen on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were hammered by concerns investors could pull back their funds out of higher-yielding emerging assets and move them back to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Apart from the Indonesian rupiah, the Mexico peso has fallen 7.5 percent so far this week, hit by Trump's threat to scrap the country's key free trade agreement with the United States and build a massive wall along the border.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real shed 5 percent on Thursday to a five-month low, while its benchmark Bovespa stock index slumped 3.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won fell to its lowest level in more than four months on the dollar's strength and concerns about Trump's foreign policy and his commitment to security in East Asia.\par \par \uc2 Markets are expecting the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  to go ahead with a rate hike in December after U.S. markets quickly stabilised from the initial Trump election shock.\par \par \uc2 The money market futures are pricing in about 75 percent chance of a rate hike.\par \par \uc2 In a remarkable shift of sentiment, the market is also now starting to price in a chance of a rate hike by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since 2011.\par \par \uc2 Debt yields are rising in Europe as well, with 10-year German Bunds yield hitting an eight-month high of 0.32 percent .\par \par \uc2 The 10-year yield in Italy, rocked by concerns Prime Minister \uc2 Matteo Renzi\uc2  may resign if he loses the Dec 4 referendum on constitutional reform he pushed for, shot up to 1.95 percent, its highest level in 14 months.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, oil prices eased as the market looked to whether \uc2 OPEC\uc2  will decide later this month to cut production to address long-running over supply concerns.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude futures fell 0.4 percent to $44.48 per barrel. (Additional reporting by Nichola Saminather in Singapore; Editing by Kim Coghill & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-11T07:54:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161111ecbb000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
