\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Resilient, but Risks Remain -- Barron's Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 By ARJEN VAN DIJKHUIZEN   \par \uc2 1,501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 08:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Growth falls to six-year low in 2015, but slowdown is only marginal ...\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets were faced with significant turmoil and capital outflows last year against the background of China's bumpy transition and the first Fed rate hike in nine years. Indonesia was deemed to be relatively vulnerable amongst emerging markets, partly reflecting its external and strong ties with China. \uc2 ABN AMRO\uc2  classified Indonesia as one of the EMs most at risk last year, while the country was classified as one of the so-called fragile five during the taper tantrum back in 2013. All this was also reflected in Indonesia's growth rate, which fell to a six-year low of 4.8% driven down by weak exports feeding into a contraction of the mining sector. Still, although this growth rate was clearly below the 6-6.5% reached in 2010-2012, it was only a touch lower than the 5.0% registered in 2014 as the slowdown was cushioned by an increase in government spending.\par \par \uc2 ...as government consumption compensates for weak exports\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's growth rate even accelerated to 5.0% year-on-year in Q4, compared to 4.7% in Q3. As in Q3, growth was mainly driven by domestic demand, particularly by government spending. In the second half of 2015, with a significant delay, the government started to spend the budgetary savings stemming from the fuel subsidy cuts in late 2014. The infrastructure budget was raised by 50%. Government spending was up by 7.3% year-on-year in Q4 (Q3: 7.1%). This fiscal stimulus was also positive for fixed investment, accelerating to 6.9% year-on-year in Q4 (Q3: 4.8%). Meanwhile, private consumption was quite resilient growing 5% year-on-year in Q4 (as in Q3), partly supported by falling inflation and improving consumer sentiment. By contrast, exports weakened further driven down by weak external demand and lower export prices, contracting by 6.4% year-on-year in Q4 (Q3: -0.6%). Meanwhile, imports contracted by 8.1% year-on-year in Q4 (Q3: -5.9%).\par \par \uc2 Construction and services compensate for contraction in mining sector\par \par \uc2 Looking at the supply side, construction and services were the outperforming sectors in the second half of this year, fuelled by higher infrastructure spending and resilient domestic demand. By contrast, the mining sector contracted by 5.4% year-on-year in 2015, as weak external demand and lower prices left their mark. Meanwhile, activity in the agricultural sectors (including forestry and fisheries) was driven down in Q3 and Q4 by bad weather conditions (El Nino, haze).\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing PMI remains below 50, but points to bottoming out\par \par \uc2 The Manufacturing PMI has remained below the neutral 50 mark since September 2014, pointing to ongoing weakness in the manufacturing sector as new orders (both domestic and external) continue to fall. While the index fell to 48.7 in February (January: 48.9), it has improved from the lower levels seen during 2015. The fact that manufacturing production fell at the weakest pace in the current 17-month phase of contraction also points to some bottoming out. While external demand remains weak, the export sub-index has also clearly improved from the low levels reached in the first part of 2015.\par \par \uc2 Reforms still proceeding gradually\par \par \uc2 In our previous Indonesia Watch, Still many challenges in 2016, published last December we concluded that the reform momentum had improved under President \uc2 Joko Widodo\uc2  (Jokowi), but that several political complexities had prevented the structural breakthrough that foreign investors had hoped for. The government has taken steps to strengthen the infrastructure and improve the business climate. This is illustrated for instance by the improvement of Indonesia's Ease of Doing Business ranking from 120 (out of 189) in 2014 to 109 in 2016, although this rank is still not very impressive. Indonesia scores much better on the Global Competitiveness Index, with a rank of 37/140. In line with the goals of strengthening infrastructure and manufacturing and promoting tourism, the government opened up 35 new business categories for foreign direct investment last month. However, the government also introduced new restrictions to foreign business to protect SMEs, while politically sensitive sectors such as agriculture and mining continue to be restricted.\par \par \uc2 Despite recent uptick, inflation has fallen to low levels ...\par \par \uc2 With the effects of the November 2015 cuts in fuel subsidies having faded out, inflation has dropped significantly in late 2015, reaching a five-year low of 3.4% in December. Since then, headline inflation has risen somewhat again, driven up by food prices, reaching 4.4% year-on-year in February. Meanwhile, core inflation has remained at a historically low level of 3.6% year-on-year in recent months. We expect average annual inflation to fall from 6.4% in 2015 to 5% this year, although upside risks could stem from renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation (we expect a moderate weakening of the rupiah versus the US dollar in the course of this year), El Nino effects on food prices and developments on global commodity markets.\par \par \uc2 ...encouraging \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  to cut policy rates twice\par \par \uc2 With inflation falling back within its target band of 3-5%, \uc2 Bank Indonesia (BI) \uc2 has followed-up by cutting the benchmark interest rates by 25 bp each in its January and February meetings. Improved market conditions and the strengthening of the rupiah (by 5% versus the US dollar since end-2015), also made BI more confident that these moves were justified. In its February meeting, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also lowered its bank reserve requirements by 1%-point, to 6.5%. We see room for one or two rate cuts in the course of this year, although we expect that BI will remain 'data-dependent' and will take the wider market environment (including monetary policy direction in the large economies) aboard as well.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth to remain at 5% in 2016, accelerating to 5.5% in 2017\par \par \uc2 While the external picture is still weak given growth disappointment in advanced economies and China's ongoing gradual slowdown, we expect the Indonesian economy will be supported by ongoing fiscal and monetary stimulus. Fiscal indicators are sound, with the budget deficit at around 2% of GDP and public debt below 30% of GDP. Hence, government consumption, including infrastructure spending, can continue to support the economy. Meanwhile - as explained above - there is some more room left for monetary easing as well. All in all, we expect economic growth to remain around 5% in 2016, accelerating to 5.5% in 2017 when the global economy should improve somewhat.\par \par \uc2 External position: risks have fallen, but not disappeared\par \par \uc2 Reflecting the overall weakness in global demand and also lower prices, both export and import growth have remained deeply in negative territory in recent months. The contraction in goods exports reached a six-year low of -20.7% year-on-year in January (average contraction in 2015: 15%). Imports also remain sharply in negative territory (-17.1% year-on-year in January), although some bottoming out seem to have taken place in recent months (average contraction in 2015 was 20%). Compared to the taper tantrum in 2013, the current account deficit has halved, although it has risen to USD 5.1 bn in Q4-2015. In GDP terms, the current account deficit has fallen more moderately, from -3.2% GDP in 2013 to around 2% last year, and is expected to rise again to around 2.5% of GDP in 2016-17. Meanwhile, after recovering sharply since the taper tantrum, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have lost around 12% since the latest peak in February 2015. This partly reflects \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the rupiah. \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's debt to export ratio has risen to 1.7 in 2015, while the share of external debt denominated in US dollar is relatively high compared to other EMs. All in all, while the external situation has improved compared to the taper tantrum episode, Indonesia still faces external financial risks.\par \par \uc2 Conclusion: Indonesia has shown resilience, but risks remain\par \par \uc2 To conclude, the external and political risks that explained why Indonesia had been classified amongst the fragile five back in 2013 have eased, but not disappeared. Economic growth has been quite resilient last year despite the weak global backdrop, mainly thanks to higher government spending. Some structural reforms have been taken under Jokowi, but more is needed. Moreover, Indonesia faces a couple of external vulnerabilities with regard to external financing, which may hit the country once more should market sentiment turn sour again. These vulnerabilities were also reflected in the development of the exchange rate and the sovereign CDS premium last year, although we have seen a clear rebound in recent months as markets have priced out Fed rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 To be considered for this feature please submit material to: Mail: asiaresearch@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Comments? E-mail us at asia.editors@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 10, 2016 02:30 ET (07:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160310ec3a0010b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict