\par\par \b \uc2 Bull Flattening, Abenomics and the Yen--It's All Connected -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,935 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2016\par 03:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's extra easing steps have become increasingly realistic due to recent stock market weakness and the yen's strength, giving a push to so-called "bull flattening" of the yield curve, in which longer-dated yields fall faster than shorter-ones in Japan, says SMBC Nikko chief strategist Chotaro Morita in a morning note. Yesterday, the 10-year Japan government bond yield (now at 0.235%) briefly hit an all-time low of 0.190% (below a low of 0.195% set on 20 January 2015), tracking the Nikkei's drop below 17,000 on Thursday. The difference with last year's bull flattening is that there is a high possibility this year that the stock market will keep dropping, inducing BOJ easing action, especially as the Nikkei is just above 17,000, or what is considered a threshold marking success or failure for the pro-growth policies known as Abenomics.\par \par \uc2  Even if the BOJ stands pat at its policy meeting on January 28-29, bond market players will likely continue to anticipate a BOJ easing bias so long as Tokyo stocks (Nikkei now up 0.6% at 17,349) and the USD/JPY (now at 117.95) stay in the doldrums. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0147 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/USD daily chart isn't quite inspiring right now, unless it ends Friday out of the 1.0793-1.1017 range that has it locked into a lifeless shuffle. The pair needs to break out of the consolidation range on the daily chart, defined by the Ichimoku Cloud chart pattern, in order to increase technical momentum. A Friday close below 1.0829, or above 1.0949, could help, by triggering either the Bollinger downtrend or uptrend channels and thus increase the odds that EUR/USD can bust its way out of the Cloud consolidation zone sooner. Fundamentally, the cue for EUR/USD is likely to come from stock markets. If equities can build on Wall Street's overnight jump, risk appetite will return more convincingly and thus depress EUR/USD, due to its funding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  characteristics - it falls when markets are putting on risk. EUR/USD is now 1.0874 from its Thursday close of 1.0864. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0147 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Another set of low inflation outcomes is in prospect in Australia in the fourth quarter. CBA's chief economist, Michael Blythe, forecasts the headline CPI rising by 0.3% on-quarter, keeping annual growth at 1.6% on-year, well below the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2-3% target band. The very low headline outcome will reflect a large detraction from the volatile petrol and fruit & vegetables components. Petrol prices were down by more than 6% in the quarter, he said. The more policy\u8209\'20\'11relevant measure of underlying inflation should average out 2.0% on-year in the quarter. This would be consistent with the RBA's current forecast trajectory, he adds. Blythe said that while low inflation will give the RBA room to cut again, the broader economy is looking chirpy and so it will stay sidelined at a policy meeting in February. Data is due out on Jan. 27 (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0140 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Indonesia rupiah is slightly stronger Friday at 13,870 to the U.S. dollar, from its last close of 13,905, despite yesterday's terrorist attacks in the capital of Jakarta and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cutting its base interest rate by 0.25 percentage points. Despite the two rupiah-negative events on the same day, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  performed admirably, only slipping a tad, possibly due to suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was suspected of selling U.S. dollars Thursday, said traders, in the aftermath of the terrorism-related bombings in Jakarta, which could have triggered a kneejerk selloff for the rupiah. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rate-cut decision, not entirely unexpected, could have weakened the rupiah due to the diminished yield-earning potential for foreign investors. Slowing economic growth and weakening inflation made BI's case for policy-easing clearer. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0135 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Singapore's corporate bond market may see more defaults or distressed exchanges and the recent turbulence is unlikely to subside in the next 12 months at least, warns \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Earlier this month, Indonesia-based mobile-phone retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 PT Trikomsel Oke\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TRIO.JK) said its obligations to pay its creditors will be suspended while it works out a restructuring. This was reported to be the first default in the Singapore bond market since 2009. Moreover, the market may see its second default in less than three months as fishing company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pacific Andes Resources Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  (P11.SG) may not honor some obligations on its SGD-denominated 200 million ($139 million), 8.5% bonds due 2017, says S&P. "In an active search for yield, Singapore investors have turned to riskier bonds over the past seven years. This trend was shaped and fueled by low interest rates since the global financial crisis and the absence of corporate defaults," said S&P. There are currently close to 400 issues outstanding in the Singapore corporate bond market (excluding government agencies). Bonds worth about S$5 billion are coming due this year and S$8 billion are due in 2017. According to estimates by S&P, rated bonds stood for less than 15% of corporates issuances, both in terms of volume and value over 2010-2015.(carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0134 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Despite the rise in finance for Australian property investors in November of +0.7%, the weaker trend in investor finance is consistent with evidence elsewhere of softer housing market conditions in the closing months of 2015, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Unusually strong growth in investor finance over recent years, where it exceeded lending to owner-occupiers for a time, is now unwinding, says Phil O'Donaghoe, economist at Deutsche. The investor share of new housing finance, by value, is now around 44.1%, broadly in line with its average since 2000 of 42.3%, he adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter: @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0133 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  has assessed the potential fallout from a closure of the Karara iron-ore mine on freight hauler Aurizon (AZJ.AU). Earlier this week, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gindalbie Metals\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GBG.AU) said Chinese joint-venture partner Ansteel is reviewing the viability of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and could withdraw funding support. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  believes the strategic review will likely include the mine owners seeking to renegotiate service contracts. "Aurizon is the rail hauler for the mine with seven years of contract remaining," the broker says. "In the worst case scenario that the mine is closed, our EPS estimates would fall 2%-3% and fair value would fall 10 cents to $4.9 per share." Still, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  retains an outperform call and A$5.00/share price target. AZJ last traded at A$3.91. (david.winning@wsj.com; Twitter: @dwinningWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0129 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shifted the yuan's daily benchmark to the U.S. dollar marginally weaker Friday. The recent changes in the daily USD/CNY parity rate have been so minute it plots almost a straight line on the charts. Friday's fixing of 6.5637 versus yesterday's 6.5616 represents a 0.03% weakening of the yuan. On Thursday, the yuan was adjusted 0.02% stronger to the greenback. As traders surmise from recent yuan fixings and suspected market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that yuan volatility is all but gone for now, focus is on China's stock market as a leading indicator. The benchmark Shanghai composite index on Thursday overcame early weakness to close 2.0% higher and above the 3,000 round-figure trading barrier, but if it slips back under that psychological watermark today, investor bearishness may return. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0123 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities are treading water until China makes clear how it will manage the value of the yuan traded in foreign markets. Hang Seng Index futures were down 0.4% then fell flat. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which just last month gave the yuan an elite status as a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said overnight that China needs to give "more clarity and communication around the exchange rate regime." The comments echoed what many equity investors had already been saying, that China's recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up its stock markets and the value of the yuan offshore were secretive and confusing. The uncertainty has made investors skittish. The Hong Kong dollar sank in the biggest intraday drop in more than a decade yesterday, which suggests that fund money is flowing out of Hong Kong, a flight that could continue Friday. (dominique.fong@wsj.com; @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0122 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Consultant Hamilton Place Strategies, which represents banks and industry trade group the Financial Services Forum, said pre-GOP debate it hopes candidates "get the basics right." "Anyone with 10 minutes and an Internet connection can see that banks are safer, with capital levels twice pre-crisis levels," said Hamilton Place Partner Tony Fratto in a statement. "Some things just aren't debatable." The firm added specifics on why Glass-Steagall wouldn't have prevented the crisis, detailing examples of big banks shrinking rather than growing and large banks capital levels. Earlier this week the WSJ reported that some at big banks were divided on how to respond to candidates' on the right and left they viewed as inaccurately bashing large financial firms. (emily.glazer@wsj.com; Twitter: @EmilyGlazerWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14 Jan 2016 21:04 ET \par Bull Flattening, Abenomics and the Yen--It's All -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0105 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Despite yesterday's technical rebound, the NZD/USD pair is still under pressure below its key resistance at 0.6510. The upward potential is likely to be limited by this threshold. Furthermore, the 50-period moving average is still declining, which suggests that the downside prevails. In that case, as long as 0.6510 holds on the upside, look for a new pullback to test 0.6425 (the previous swing low), if breakout, a further drop is more likely toward 0.6400. This content was produced exclusively for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  by Trading Central (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2  ), an investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0103 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The GBP/USD pair managed to hold above its nearest support at 1.4350, which should call for a stabilization. The 20-period moving average is turning up and has just crossed above the 50-period one. The relative strength index jumped above its neutrality area at 50 and is now displaying strong upward momentum. As long as 1.4350 is not broken, the pair is likely to advance to 1.4475 & 1.4515 in extension. Alternatively, below 1.4350 look for further downsides with 1.4300 & 1.4225 as targets. This content was produced exclusively for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  by Trading Central (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2  ), an investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 14, 2016 21:04 ET (02:04 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160115ec1f0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\pics