\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Rupiah Holds on to Strength After Rate Cut -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2016\par 02:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Indonesia rupiah is slightly stronger Friday at 13,870 to the U.S. dollar, from its last close of 13,905, despite yesterday's terrorist attacks in the capital of Jakarta and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cutting its base interest rate by 0.25 percentage points. Despite the two rupiah-negative events on the same day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  performed admirably, only slipping a tad, possibly due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market. \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of selling U.S. dollars Thursday, said traders, in the aftermath of the terrorism-related bombings in Jakarta, which could have triggered a kneejerk selloff for the rupiah. \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's rate-cut decision, not entirely unexpected, could have weakened the rupiah due to the diminished yield-earning potential for foreign investors. Slowing economic growth and weakening inflation made BI's case for policy-easing clearer. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 14, 2016 20:40 ET (01:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160115ec1f00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
