\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia's JCI Fell After Explosions Hit Jakarta -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2016\par 07:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0622 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Jakarta Composite Index fell for the few minutes of trading before the midday break, after deadly blasts hit Jakarta Thursday afternoon. The index was last down 1.7% to 4459.32, the worst performer in Southeast Asia, with losses concentrated in some of the firms that have also been the worst-hit from recent volatility related to China. \uc2 Media Nusantara Citra\uc2  (MNCN.JK) falls 6.1%, while cement companies \uc2 Indocement Tunggal Prakarsa\uc2  (INTP.JK) and \uc2 Semen Indonesia\uc2  (SMGR.JK) are down 3.5% and 5.1%m respectively. Of 521 listed companies, 210 fall and only 38 gain, with the rest unchanged. The market will reopen shortly for the afternoon session. (jake.watts@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @jmwatts_) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Indonesian rupiah fell as much as 1% against the U.S. dollar after multiple explosions hit Jakarta. The USD/IDR hit as high as 13960, and is now hovering at 13940--down 0.9% from 13820 late Wednesday. The Indian rupee also tumbled after the news of blast, with the USD/INR once hitting the lowest since Sept 2013. Even before the blast, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Singapore dollar to Vietnam dong are weaker following stock rout in the U.S. overnight, which in turn tanked Asian stocks on Thursday. The Philippines peso slides more than peers, with a 0.5% drop against the U.S. dollar, while the yuan in offshore market plummets 0.6% against the greenback. (fiona.law@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0544 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The India rupee is weaker versus the U.S. dollar, mirroring the depreciation of other emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday, due to general risk aversion. USD/INR has risen to 67.20--to its highest level since September 2013--amid market pessimism tied to persistently falling crude oil prices, and fears that China's economy and financial markets are in a precarious state. The rupee, like other high-yield \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is susceptible to foreign investor outflows as losses mount in other regions and asset classes. Traders tend to trim all positions when their chips are down, to cut risk and to re-assess the situation. Wednesday's steep drop in U.S. stocks, coupled with a rally in safe haven assets such as Treasurys and gold, suggest this is such a time when investors might take some money off the table. USD/INR is now 67.14 from its Wednesday close of 66.91. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0542 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese stocks extend their falls with the benchmark Nikkei dipping below 17000 earlier, weighed on by uncertainty over earnings and recent instability of Chinese markets, says Shigeo Sugawara, senior investment officer at Sompo Japan Nipponkoa Asset Management. The Nikkei is down 3.6% at 17077.08, getting closer to the recent low of 16901.49 marked on Sept 29. Recent falls in commodity prices and the yen's renewed strength are raising some concerns that Japanese companies might have to revise their earnings projections downward. Sugawara says once corporate earnings for the previous quarter show steady results, markets will likely to rebound. "So far we haven't seen much deterioration of fundamentals, and only the worsened sentiment is causing gyrations...The key is whether we can confirm [solid corporate] fundamentals, until then the sentiment is likely dictate trading," he says.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0537 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China needs to step up communication to investors on how it regulates markets, says a fund manager. Recent moves have been confusing, such as circuit breaker rules that exacerbated the sell-off last week in Chinese markets. The circuit breakers "were not necessarily a bad idea, but the limits of trading were a bit too tight for emerging markets," says Philip Li, senior fund manager of the Value Partners Classic Fund, which manages $1.6 billion of assets. He was referring to the tight gap between the first threshold for a 15-minute freeze in Chinese stock trading--a 5% drop--and the second level, a 7% plunge. Then they were abruptly scrapped. "I agree that the level of regulation and the control has been a blow to the reputation in terms of the regulators. Clearly, an improved level of transparency would definitely help markets." (dominique.fong@wsj.com; @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0531 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's minister in charge of economic planning assures investors that "high multiplier" infrastructure projects in Malaysia will not face cuts when the government reviews its national budget next week, says AllianceDBS Research. "[The] Minister assuaged investors [concerns] that government will not cut its spending plans at the expense of economic growth, and high multiplier mega projects such as MTR Line 2 will proceed as planned," said the research house, which had hosted Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Abdul Wahid Omar\f28 \uc2 \f2  at a luncheon talk. Construction companies present at the event said contract flow will remain healthy in 2016, said AllianceDBS. The 28 billion ringgit ($6.4 billion) Mass Rapid Transit Line 2 is among the projects in the pipeline this year. Others are the MYR9 billion ringgit \f28 \uc2 \f2 Light Rail Transit\f28 \uc2 \f2  Line 3 and some expressways. The house's top picks of stocks include construction companies Gamuda (5398.KU) (Buy). Muhibbah (5703.KU) (Buy) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kimlun Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (5171.KU) (Buy). (celine.fernandez@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0525 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The spike earlier this week in the Hong Kong interbank rate for offshore yuan "is not indicative of a broader liquidity problem in the Hong Kong banking system," analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note on Thursday. Yuan-denominated lending is a small business for banks such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard Chartered\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Citi, and the pool of liquidity is big enough for clients so that the spike shouldn't crimp lending and is "unlikely to result in higher impairment charges" for Hong Kong banks, according to Fitch. The question that does remain is whether the yuan can be considered a viable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  internationally if such events continue to occur, bankers and analysts say. (julie.steinberg@wsj.com; @jbsteins) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0514 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The benchmark 10-year JGB yield briefly hits its all-time low of 0.190%, with the selloff in Tokyo stocks in mid-afternoon trade (benchmark Nikkei down 17000, 1st time since Sept. 29 2015) lifting demand for safe haven assets. The yield is now back to 0.205% after ratcheting down to that low, even lower than the previous record low of 0.195% set on Jan. 20 2015. Given unstable Asian stock markets and a falloff in oil prices, "we still don't see investors willing to take risks in a positive manner," says Masahiro Ichikawa, senior strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui Asset Management. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0511 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] New Zealand shares followed global equities lower on Thursday as volatility returned to markets. After a horror start to the year, the New Zealand market enjoyed two positive sessions on Tuesday and Wednesday, but those gains were undone on Thursday, with the benchmark NZX-50 index losing 0.69%, or 43 points, to close at 6109.3. The falls were driven by offshore losses, triggered by a volatile session on Wall Street, which saw the Dow Jones Industrial index lose 2.2% and the S&P 500 drop 2.5%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Peter Young\f28 \uc2 \f2  from Forsyth Barr said a lot of the selling on the New Zealand market was sentiment driven following the rout on overseas markets, concerns about China and falling oil prices. "Our market will be held up to a certain extent because it pays good dividends, but really we're just following what's happening offshore at the moment." \f28 \uc2 \f2 The a2 Milk Company\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ATM.NZ) lost 2.78% to NZ$1.75 after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  today maintained its underperform rating on the stock. (kate.geenty@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0510 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indonesia's rupiah fell against the U.S. dollar on news reports of explosions and gunfire in the capital Jakarta. The risk-sensitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  extended its earlier 0.6% loss to fall as much as 1.0% against the greenback Thursday, but appears to be stabilizing. A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader said it seems that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-owned banks to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The rupiah and the domestic stock market were already sliding early Thursday, due to overnight risk aversion related to oil price weakness, but slipped further on news of bomb blasts and casualties in the central business district of Indonesia capital Jakarta. The benchmark Jakarta stock index is now down 1.7% from an earlier 1.2% decline. USD/IDR is now 13,950 from its Wednesday close of 13,820. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0505 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Security Bank Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (SECB.PH) are moving higher Thursday--bucking the weakness of the broader market--after it agreed to sell a 20% stake to \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi UFJ Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The deal will inject 36.9 billion pesos ($782 million) of fresh capital into Security Bank. The MUFJ paid an 80% premium to the Philippine lender's stock price. Aside from the fresh capital, the deal will also provide Security Bank access to the Japanese market. (Cris.Larano@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0500 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China says Thursday that it won't impose tighter \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rules on individual foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales to restrict purchases of the U.S. dollar, which has been getting stronger against the yuan. But economists say China can't be totally hands-off in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as long as capital flight remains a worry. China can't keep burning through its official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to support the yuan after reserves lost $108 billion in December, a record monthly drop, say \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists. The most China can afford to lose, at most, is $500 billion per year for another few years, they estimate. Obviously the rate must slow. "Therefore, we believe China will have to, in the meantime, rely on an indirect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  approach," they say. How? By continuing to restrict yuan conversions into other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , remittances, overseas investments and yuan swap agreements. (dominique.fong@wsj.com; @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 14, 2016 01:22 ET (06:22 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160114ec1e000dw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
