\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  buffer smaller than it looks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan and Gayatri Suroyo   \par \uc2 904 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2015\par 06:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE/JAKARTA, Nov 30 (Reuters) - Indonesia's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have doubled in size in the eight years since the global financial crisis roiled its markets but an examination by Reuters of its reserve assets shows a thinner buffer than officially reported data on gross reserves suggests.\par \par \uc2 More than a quarter of \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's (BI) reserves include borrowings from banks and market participants, a much higher proportion than those of other Asian central banks.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say that as borrowings, they are actually short-term liabilities rather than reserve assets, and so cannot be used for market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These analysts say this constrains the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BI rejects this argument and stresses that its markets are safe. The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) told Reuters it is unable to make a conclusive call one way or the other because details of capital inflows, which could offset these borrowings, were unknown.\par \par \uc2 Official \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data shows gross reserves amounted to $101.7 billion at the end of September, double the levels during the 2008 global financial crisis and several times its meagre reserves in 1998. Reserves dropped slightly to $100.7 billion in October.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2 's breakdown, available only for September, shows $28.7 billion of that is made up of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans, deposits and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, or what the \uc2 IMF\uc2  terms "pre-determined short-term net drains" on reserves.\par \par \uc2 That effectively leaves \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with about $73 billion of net or useable reserves in a worst-case-scenario in which foreign lenders and investors lose faith and flee rupiah assets. That amount just meets the traditional criterion for reserves adequacy of six months' worth of imports.\par \par \uc2 "Technically these items should be included in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," said \uc2 Sanjay Mathur\uc2 , \uc2 RBS\uc2 's head of Asian economic research and strategy, referring to the bank deposits and loans included in the BI's reserves. "But they are not a part of the useable, unencumbered reserves. It's a technical distinction."\par \par \uc2 In terms of such "useable" reserves, Indonesia's are the lowest among its Asian peers.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is Asia's biggest underperformer this year, had gross reserves of $93.3 billion at the end of September and net reserves of $85 billion. \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has $51 billion of forward net sold dollar positions, which means its useable reserves are that much higher than the gross total $368 billion.\par \par \uc2 "It's somewhat of a concern in as far as it shows that the Indonesia's reserve adequacy is lower in net terms," said Claudio Piron, co-head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rates strategy at \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It just means it's more vulnerable among its Asian peer group, particularly against any external negative shock."\par \par \uc2 THE VULNERABLE RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 BI officials said the pre-determined short-term drains were maturing government loans, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accepts from banks.\par \par \uc2 Its executive director for monetary and economic policy, Juda Agung, told Reuters some of the deposits would be rolled over and there would be new loans, to replace these claims.\par \par \uc2 "We are safe. We don't have any problem from the sufficiency side," Agung said.\par \par \uc2 "It is (part of) the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, whether it is considered 'net' position or above. It can be used for anything, including monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The IMF's \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  representative, Benedict Bingham, said there wasn't a straightforward interpretation for these pre-determined drains on reserves, barring the portion that was from deposits placed by banks with BI.\par \par \uc2 If those deposits were not rolled over, "then Indonesia's reserves will go down. By extension though, if they are rolled over, then reserves won't go down," Bingham said.\par \par \uc2 Bingham also said the impact of other "pre-determined net drains", for example principal and interest payments on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, was more difficult to judge because it depended on whether there would be inflows to offset these payments.\par \par \uc2 "You can't simply say that portion of reserves won't be available for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . You need to make a broader judgment on the balance of payments," he said.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah has lost about 10 percent of its value this year against the dollar as investors pulled money out of one of Asia's highest-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A string of measures from BI and falling fuel import prices have helped put a floor under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  although it remains vulnerable to any rise in U.S. interest rates and faltering demand for resource exports, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Indonesia's useable \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves cushion puts it in a precarious position, say some analysts, given foreigners own about a third or $39 billion worth of its outstanding bonds. Plus, short-term debt accounts for about a seventh of its $304.5 billion external debt.\par \par \uc2 "The ability of Indonesia to necessarily defend itself is weakened," said Piron.\par \par \uc2 "The positive side of that is that their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not overwhelmingly overvalued so there is no danger of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis as such. And it is a free floating exchange rate, so better positioned to deal with the weakness."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Randy Fabi in Jakarta; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-11-30T06:30:31.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151130ebbu006bz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
