\par\par \b \uc2 TEXT-Indonesia  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  holds benchmark rate\b0\par\par\uc2 1,336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2015\par 12:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Nov 17 (Reuters) - \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  on Tuesday held its benchmark policy rate at 7.50 percent, but cut banks' reserve requirement.\par \par \uc2 Here is the text of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement:\par \par \uc2 The BI Board of Governors agreed on 17th November 2015 to hold the BI Rate at 7.50 percent, while maintaining the Deposit Facility rate at 5.50 percent and the Lending facility rate at 8.00 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Board of Governors also decided to lower the primary reserve requirement in Rupiah from 8.0 percent to 7.50 percent, effective on 1st December 2015.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  considered that the macroeconomic stability has continued to improve, making room to maintain a loose monetary policy stance.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  believes that the 2015 inflation will be maintained at the lower end of the 4\u177\'00\'B11 percent inflation target, with the current account deficit is projected at 2 percent of GDP.\par \par \uc2 With the lingering uncertainty in the global financial market, stemming mainly from the expected Federal Funds Rate (FFR) hike as well as the diversity of monetary policies from \uc2 ECB\uc2 , BoJ, and PBoC, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will remain vigilant in loosening its monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 In this respect, lowering the primary reserve requirement is expected to boost bank financing capacity to support escalating economic activity during the third quarter and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Moving forward, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will consistently maintain coordination with the Government in order to reinforce the structure of the economy, thereby facilitating stronger economic growth and preserving macroeconomic and financial system stability.\par \par \uc2 The global recovery remains uneven, while pressures on global financial markets demand vigilance.\par \par \uc2 The US growth was moderate in line with limited manufacturing gains and a weak export. Conversely, the labour sector showed signs of improvement, with lower unemployment along with rising income growth and nonfarm payroll. Consequently, such conditions reignited expectations of an imminent FFR hike in December 2015.\par \par \uc2 The economic recoveries of Europe and Japan are still considered weak, driving the two countries to perform loose monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 China's economic slowdown, as indicated by the PMI contraction along with a decrease in exports, has also led to a loose monetary policy. China's government, in addition, is also conducting financial market reform, as well as renmimbi internationalisation.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth accelerated in the third quarter of 2015, with the trend projected to continue into the fourth quarter. Third quarter growth was recorded at 4.73 percent (yoy), surpassing that posted last period at 4.67 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 This was mainly due to stronger government consumption and investment, in line with significant progress in terms of government infrastructure projects. In addition, government capital spending increased by 38.8 percent by October 2015.\par \par \uc2 Household consumption remains strong, as reflected by an improved purchasing power.\par \par \uc2 From an external standpoint, however, persistently low commodity prices and sluggish growth in trade partner countries, such as the United States, China and Singapore, precipitated a deeper contraction in exports from Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 By region, economic conditions on the island of Java improved, while limited growth was reported on Sumatra. Conversely, economic growth in Eastern Indonesia slowed, while Kalimantan experienced negative growth for the first time in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  expects the national economic grains to endure into the fourth quarter as government infrastructure projects gain momentum.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, private investment is predicted to increase due to the recent Government policy packages, including deregulations to support the investment climate. In general, domestic economic growth was projected at the lower end of the 4.7-5.1 percent range for 2015, and is expected to increase to 5.2-5.6 percent on 2016.\par \par \uc2 A sound non-oil and gas trade balance preserved the current account gains during the reporting period. The current account deficit in the Indonesia Balance of Payments (BoP) stood at $4.0 billion (1.86 percent of GDP) in Q3/2015, improving from $7.0 billion (3.02 percent of GDP) in Q3/2014 and $4.2 billion (1.95 percent of GDP) in Q2/2015.\par \par \uc2 The improvements stemmed from gains in the non-oil and gas trade balance as imports decreased sharply in line with limited domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, non-oil and gas exports experienced a less pronounced decline due to lower commodity prices, despite positive growth in real terms. The oil and gas trade deficit remained stable as a smaller gas surplus was offset by a smaller oil deficit.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Indonesia trade balance recorded a positive development, with a surplus of $1.01 billion in October 2015.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the capital and financial account also maintained a surplus despite widespread uncertainty overshadowing global financial markets. The surplus declined, however, compared to the previous period and was insufficient to fully offset the current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, the position of reserve assets stood at $100.7 billion at the end of October 2015, equivalent to 7.1 months of imports or 6.6 months of imports and servicing public external debt, which is well above the international adequacy standard of around three months.\par \par \uc2 The Rupiah rebounded after intense depreciatory pressures were felt in the third quarter. In Q3/2015, the Rupiah depreciated on average by 5.35 percent (qtq) to a level of 13,873 per dollar due to external factors, namely concerns over the normalisation policy of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as well as Yuan devaluation in China.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the Rupiah strengthened in October 2015 on positive sentiment for EM due to a dovish announcement relayed at the FOMC, coupled with optimism regarding the economic outlook of Indonesia after the Government released a series of policy packages and \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilise the Rupiah.\par \par \uc2 Rupiah appreciation averaged 4.47 percent (mtm) to a level of 13,783 per dollar. Moving forward, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will continue to maintain exchange rate stability in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fundamental value.\par \par \uc2 The Consumer Price Index (CPI) experienced deflation in October 2015, bringing national inflation for the year to within the target corridor of 4\u177\'00\'B11 percent. Deflation was recorded at 0.08 percent (mtm) or 6.25 percent (yoy) in line with volatile food deflation after foodstuff prices were corrected due to increased supply.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, inflation from January to October 2015 stood at 2.16 percent (ytd) or 6.25 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 Volatile food deflation was backed by an increase in foodstuff supply. Furthermore, core inflation and administered prices were also considered low compared to historical trends. Core inflation was recorded at 0.23 percent (mtm) or 5.02 percent (yoy) in line with Rupiah appreciation, limited domestic demand and anchored inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 Administered price inflation remained low, contributed by cheaper diesel prices and the knock-on effect of lower 12 kg LPG canisters since September 2015.\par \par \uc2 Based on conditions through to October 2015, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  confirmed that inflation in 2015 will fall towards the lower end of the 4\u177\'00\'B11 percent target, supported by strong policy coordination with the government to control inflation locally and nationally. In addition, low inflation through to October 2015 was also indicative of maintained price stability.\par \par \uc2 Financial system stability remained solid, underpinned by a resilient banking system and relatively stable financial markets. Banking industry resilience endured, with credit, liquidity and market risks well mitigated. In September 2015, the Capital Adequacy Ratio (CAR) remained well above the 8 percent minimum threshold at 20.4 percent, while non-performing loans (NPL) were low and stable at 2.7 percent (gross) or 1.3 percent (net).\par \par \uc2 In terms of the intermediation function, credit growth was recorded at 11.1 percent(yoy), higher than that of the previous month, while deposit growth on September 2015 was recorded at 11.7 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 Looking forward, credit growth is predicted to continue accelerating in line with an increase in economic activity and the looser macro prudential policy stance adopted by \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 , along with the lower primary reserve requirement. (Reporting by Gayatri Suroyo and Nilufar Rizki; Editing by Sanjeev Miglani)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-11-20T11:55:24.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151117ebbh00dyv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
