\par\par \b \uc2 TEXT-\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  holds rates steady, sees easing economic pressure\b0\par\par\uc2 1,267 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2015\par 14:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Oct 15 (Reuters) - \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  held its benchmark policy rate on Thursday at 7.50 percent, a level it has maintained since February, but said it may begin easing monetary policy in the future.\par \par \uc2 Here is the text of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement:\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank Indonesia Board of Governors\uc2  decided on 15th October 2015 to hold the BI Rate at 7.50 percent, while maintaining the Deposit Facility rate at 5.50 percent and the Lending Facility rate at 8.00 percent.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  was convinced that 2015 inflation will stay within the lower half of the 4 percent target corridor, while the current account deficit was predicted at a healthier level than previously thought, namely around 2 percent of GDP at year-end.\par \par \uc2 Domestic economic growth was expected to rebound on the back of greater government capital spending despite relatively sluggish activity in the private sector.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  believes that the pressures on macroeconomic stability have eased, making room to loosen monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 However, with high uncertainty in the global market, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will remain vigilant and continue to monitor global risks despite more conducive conditions on global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, BI policy will continue to focus, in the near term, on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilisation measures by continuing to maintain the rupiah stability, strengthening rupiah liquidity management and the supply and demand of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will continue to reinforce its policy mix in order to maintain macroeconomic and financial system stability. \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  also welcomes the recent policy packages released by the government aimed at catalysing economic growth and expediting structural reforms in order to bolster domestic economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Moving forward, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will continue to strengthen policy coordination with the government to support policy consistency and efficacy as the key to a sounder economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 The global economic recovery was limited, while pressures on global financial markets have begun to ease. This is mainly a result of limited growth in the emerging markets, in particular China, where analysts predicted a further downswing on weaker manufacturing indicators and exports.\par \par \uc2 Conversely, economic growth rebounded in advanced countries but is yet to be considered solid. The U.S. recovery remained fragile due to weak employment indicators, which, when coupled with the dovish announcement relayed by the FOMC in September 2015, pushed predictions for the Fed Funds Rate hike further back.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the economy of Europe was expected to continue improving on the back of strong domestic demand and an expansive manufacturing sector.\par \par \uc2 The sluggish global recovery perpetuated the international commodity price slide.\par \par \uc2 Pressures on global financial markets began to dissipate in early October 2015, however, in line with the postponed FFR hike.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will continue to monitor global risks that could potentially trigger a portfolio capital reversal from emerging market countries, including Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 On the home front, domestic economic growth in Q3/2015 was projected to slightly outpace that posted the previous quarter due to greater capital spending by the government despite relatively sluggish private sector activity.\par \par \uc2 Government investment accelerated in line with greater capital spending, reflecting an increase in the value of government projects entering the construction phase.\par \par \uc2 Stronger sales of cement and heavy equipment further confirmed the increase in investment activity.\par \par \uc2 In addition, while relatively limited, private investment was also expected to buck its downward trend due to the policy packages introduced recently by the government, including deregulation to buoy the investment climate.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, consumption indicators, including retail sales and consumer confidence, were down but have begun to show signs of improvement with automotive sales continuing to rise.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, exports were also predicted to increase gradually in line with weaker-than-expected global growth. Economic growth was projected in the range of 4.7-5.1 percent for 2015.\par \par \uc2 Government consistency in terms of pushing structural reforms through various policy packages and the realisation of infrastructure projects are expected to drive momentum to a sounder economy.\par \par \uc2 The trade balance of Indonesia again recorded a surplus in September 2015, primarily supported by a non-oil and gas trade surplus. The trade surplus stood at $1.02 billion, exceeding the $0.33 billion posted in August 2015.\par \par \uc2 The non-oil and gas surplus was due to improvements in the non-oil and gas exports, especially manufacturing exports, amid a decline in the non-oil and gas imports, specifically those of raw materials and consumer goods. On the other hand, the oil and gas trade deficit declined in line with a sharp drop off in oil and gas imports.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the financial account, although non-resident portfolio inflows to financial markets in Indonesia began to ebb, the financial account still reached net inflow of $2.9 billion accumulatively at the end of September 2015.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve assets stood at $101.7 billion at the end of September, equivalent to 7.0 months of imports or 6.8 months of imports and servicing public external debt, which is well above the international adequacy standard of three months.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah rebounded at the beginning of October after intense depreciatory pressures were felt in September.\par \par \uc2 Rupiah appreciation stemmed from positive sentiment regarding the possible delay of the FFR hike and optimism concerning the domestic economic outlook after the government released a series of policy packages and \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The combination of policy packages spurred an influx of foreign capital to financial markets in Indonesia, which subsequently propped up the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will continue to strengthen efforts to stabilise the rupiah in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fundamental value, thereby maintaining macroeconomic and financial system stability.\par \par \uc2 The Consumer Price Index (CPI) experienced deflation in September 2015, leading to sufficiently low headline inflation from January-September 2015.\par \par \uc2 CPI deflation stood at 0.05 percent (mtm) or 6.83 percent(yoy) on an annualised basis, primarily due to volatile foods and administered prices. Consequently, headline inflation from January-September 2015 was relatively low at just 2.24 percent (ytd), providing sound evidence of stable prices.\par \par \uc2 Volatile food deflation stemmed from abundant food supply as the harvesting season persisted for a number of commodities at several production hubs.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, administered prices also experienced deflation as a result of a post-Eid correction to airfares together with lower Pertamax and Pertalite petrol prices as the international oil price continued to slump. On the other hand, core inflation was recorded at 0.44 percent (mtm) or 5.07 percent (yoy), which was down on the previous period due to ready-to-consume foods, education fees and gold jewellery.\par \par \uc2 Congruous with increasingly controlled and lower inflation, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  predicted that 2015 inflation will be below the inflation target midpoint of 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Financial system stability remained solid, underpinned by a resilient banking system and relatively stable financial markets. Banking industry resilience endured, with credit, liquidity and market risks well mitigated. In August 2015, the Capital Adequacy Ratio (CAR) remained well above the 8 percent minimum threshold at 20.5 percent, while non-performing loans (NPL) were low and stable at 2.8 percent (gross) or 1.4 percent (net).\par \par \uc2 In terms of the intermediation function, credit growth accelerated to 10.9 percent (yoy). Deposit growth in August 2015 was recorded at 13.2 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 Looking forward, credit growth is predicted to continue accelerating in line with an increase in economic activity and the looser macroprudential policy stance adopted by \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 . (Reporting by Hidayat Setiaji, Nilufar Rizki and Gayatri Suroyo)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-10-18T12:16:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151015ebaf00f4i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
