\par\par \b \uc2 TEXT-Indonesia's c.bank holds rates at 7.50 pct\b0\par\par\uc2 1,174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2015\par 13:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Sept 17 (Reuters) - \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  held its benchmark policy rate on Thursday at 7.50 percent, a level it has maintained since February in anticipation the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  could raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Here is the text of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement:\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank Indonesia (BI) \uc2 Board of Governors decided on September 17, 2015 to hold the BI Rate at 7.50 percent, while maintaining the Deposit Facility Rate at 5.50 percent and the Lending Facility Rate at 8.00 percent.\par \par \uc2 The decision is consistent with efforts to control inflation within the target corridor of 4\u177\'00\'B11 percent in 2015 and 2016.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the decision is also part of \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's measures to anticipate possibilities of Fed Fund Rate (FFR) hike.\par \par \uc2 In the near term, amidst an uncertain global economy, BI policy will continue to focus on rupiah stabilisation measures by optimising monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the rupiah and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply and demand management, while continuing to deepen the money market.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, BI constantly strengthens its macroprudential and monetary policy mix in order to maintain macroeconomic stability, in particular exchange rate and financial system stability, to bolster economic sustainability.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, BI welcomes the government's recent policy package, unveiled on September 9, 2015, aimed at propping up the flagging economy through structural reforms.\par \par \uc2 Moving forward, BI will continue to strengthen policy coordination with the government in order to support effective and consistent structural policies as the key to a sound economic outlook in Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 Despite early indications of U.S. economic momentum, global economic growth was weaker than previously projected.\par \par \uc2 The global slowdown stemmed primarily from limited growth in emerging market countries, particularly China.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economic growth is estimated to be higher than previously projected, gaining momentum on stronger consumption.\par \par \uc2 Yuan devaluation, however, could undermine U.S. exports, with inflation coming in below target.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, uncertainty concerning the proposed FFR hike in the United States lingered, with the increase now expected towards year end.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the economy of Europe was predicted to improve on robust domestic demand and future consumer confidence.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, the Chinese economy continued to moderate, despite ongoing improvements in the property sector.\par \par \uc2 Limited growth was forecast for Japan in line with sluggish domestic demand. Solid growth, however, was projected for India's economy, with a bias towards the lower end of the range.\par \par \uc2 In addition to uncertainty concerning the proposed FFR hike, global financial market risk also escalated, due to China's policy of yuan devaluation and adopting a more flexible exchange rate regime.\par \par \uc2 On the home front, the domestic economy was projected to accelerate in Q3/2015.\par \par \uc2 Household consumption has shown signs of improvement, reflecting a surge in motorcycle sales.\par \par \uc2 Investment is also expected to increase, congruous with greater government investment, as more infrastructure projects begin construction, such as toll roads, power stations and dams.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, higher cement sales, imports of capital goods and stronger credit growth are all indicative of increasing investment activity.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the absorption of regional fiscal spending could potentially increase in line with special government measures.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, exports will continue to improve gradually as the global economy recovers.\par \par \uc2 On an annualised basis, therefore, economic growth was projected in the 4.7-5.1 percent range for 2015 in line with consistent government structural reforms implemented through various economic policy packages and infrastructure projects.\par \par \uc2 The trade balance of Indonesia recorded a surplus in August 2015.\par \par \uc2 Although lower from the previous period, the trade surplus in August stood at $0.43 billion.\par \par \uc2 The decline stemmed from a narrower non-oil and gas trade surplus as imports surged of machinery and mechanical equipment, electrical equipment as well as iron and steel.\par \par \uc2 The import growth of such commodities provided early indications of increasing economic activity.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the oil and gas trade deficit narrowed on the month earlier as imports declined and exports increased.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the financial account, despite growing pressure on foreign capital inflows as global uncertainty spread, accumulative foreign portfolio inflows to financial markets in Indonesia through to August 2015 totalled $3.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, the position of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve assets at the end of August 2015 stood at $105.3 billion, equivalent to 7.1 months of imports or 6.9 months of imports and servicing public external debt, which is well above the international adequacy standard of three months.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah depreciated on the back of intense external pressures.\par \par \uc2 On August 2015, the rupiah fell by an average of 2.9 percent (mtm) to a level of 13,789 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Pressures mainly originated from the yuan devaluation policy of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  combined with uncertainty over the proposed FFR hike in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Domestically, however, demand for dollars surged in order to service foreign debt, which exacerbated the rupiah overshoot.\par \par \uc2 Addressing the aforementioned developments, \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market through rupiah stabilisation measures in accordance with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fundamental value, thereby nurturing macroeconomic and financial system stability.\par \par \uc2 In addition, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  also optimised monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage demand and bolster foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply.\par \par \uc2 Inflation fell in August 2015 on post-Eid-ul-Fitr price corrections.\par \par \uc2 CPI inflation was recorded at 0.39 percent (mtm) or 7.18 percent (yoy) in August 2015 as a result of administered price deflation and controlled core inflation.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, headline inflation for 2015 through to August stood at 2.29 percent (ytd). Core inflation was recorded at 0.52 percent (mtm) or 4.92 percent (yoy), up on the month earlier due to rising education fees and more expensive ready-to-consume foods.\par \par \uc2 Inflationary pressures were controlled by anchored expectations and domestic economic moderation. Corrections to various transport fares, especially airfares, intra-city fares and rail fares, contributed to administered price deflation.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, volatile foods experienced inflation as the prices of various foodstuffs increased.\par \par \uc2 Based on inflation through to August 2015, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  perceives the inflation target of 4\u177\'00\'B11 percent for 2015 as attainable through the backing of policy coordination to control inflation nationally and regionally.\par \par \uc2 Financial system stability remained solid, underpinned by a resilient banking system and relatively stable financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Banking industry resilience endured, with credit, liquidity and market risks well mitigated. The Capital Adequacy Ratio remained stong, well above the statutory minimum of 8 percent at 20.5 percent in July 2015.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, non-performing loans (NPL) were low and stable at 2.7 percent (gross) or 1.4 percent (net).\par \par \uc2 In terms of the intermediary function, credit growth slowed to 9.7 percent (yoy), but escalated on August, estimated to reach 10,9 percent.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, deposit growth reached 14.6 percent (yoy) on July 2015.\par \par \uc2 Credit growth was forecast to increase in upcoming periods as economic activity escalates and \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  loosens its macroprudential policy stance. (Reporting by Nilufar Rizki, Hidayat Setiaji, and Gayatri Suroyo; Editing by Clarence Fernandez)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-20T11:53:54.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150917eb9h00dqh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
