\par\par \b \uc2 USD/IDR Extends Upward March to New 17-Year Peak -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2015\par 05:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR marched higher Tuesday to a third consecutive 17-year high on persistent betting that the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  may raise interest rates this month despite a mixed U.S. jobs report on Friday. The rupiah has fallen to 14,290 against the U.S. unit - its weakest level since 1998 - despite intermittent spot market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. The broad weakness across Asian emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely makes \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  more willing to tolerate the rupiah's weakening. Regional Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have tanked to multi-year lows on generally-poor risk appetite. The continued slowdown of China's economy - evidenced by Tuesday's weaker-than-forecast August exports data - continues to be one of two main concerns for Asian investors. The other factor crimping Asian investors' outlook is the likelihood of tighter U.S. monetary policy to come. USD/IDR is now 14,290 from its Monday close of 14,245. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: MNG\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 08-09-15 0347GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150908eb980005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
