\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Hits New 17-Year Low on U.S. Rate-Rise Fears -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2015\par 03:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia's rupiah fell Monday to its weakest against the U.S. dollar since 1998 after Friday's U.S. jobs report kept alive the possibility of a U.S. interest rate rise. USD/IDR rose as high as 14,240 and now lingers just slightly beneath that record level. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the pace of the rupiah's decline can be expected; \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently said that it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to stem excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuation. The mixed U.S. jobs report showed that the unemployment rate fell to 5.1% though the number of jobs added in August was below expectations. The contradicting numbers had some analysts suggesting that the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  might wait till December to raise interest rates, but the broad rise of the greenback suggests punters are still betting on a rate-rise at the Sept. 16-17 FOMC meeting. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: MNG\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 07-09-15 0153GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150907eb970002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
