\par\par \b \uc2 Drop in Japan Shares Likely to Prompt \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Easing -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2015\par 07:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0523 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] A tumble in Tokyo share prices will increase pressure on the BOJ to implement additional easing and shore up the boost to household incomes from higher equity prices, says \uc2 Jesper Koll\uc2 , CEO of WisdomTree Japan. "The wealth effect is exactly what gets you going here," he says. "The pressure on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is going to start to increase irrespective of the wage dynamic." He says the BOJ could frame additional easing as necessary to achieve inflation amid falling global demand and commodity prices. Koll adds the BOJ could choose to buy more ETFs or buy municipal bonds in addition to the national government bonds it currently buys as part of an expanded easing program. (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0507 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] India's rupee slips further on Wednesday to 66.28 for one U.S. dollar, from 66.10 at the end of Tuesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen nearly 4% against the dollar this month, and some believe it could go down to 68 or 70 rupees for a dollar in the coming days, which would be its lowest level ever. Though \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has more than $350 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to help protect the rupee, analysts expect the bank to remain cautious in using this money. "If there's a (global) selloff pressure, you don't really fight it," says Sachchidanand Shukla, economist at Axis Capital Ltd. However, a lower rupee isn't a big problem right now, he says, as it helps make India's exports more competitive.\par \par \uc2 (shefali.anand@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0442 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Liquidity is pretty "rarefied" in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets right now and the New Zealand dollar is "jiving to the beat of Chinese equities," says Tim Kelleher, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  institutional sales at Auckland-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASB Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "It's up, down, sideways," he says. NZD/USD is at 0.6501 and has spent past 24-hours in a choppy range. "It jumped up on nothing and dropped straight back down again. It really is lacking in liquidity but I don't think its the only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lacking in liquidity right now," says Kelleher. He says markets are calmer but wary. It's all about equity markets. "Every time China has a bounce, you see a bit of risk on and every time it sells off, you see a bit of risk off," he says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0437 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The ringgit is recovering from its fresh 17-year low notched early Wednesday, after Malaysia's sovereign investment fund 1MDB denied media reports that Abu Dhabi's \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Petroleum Investment Co\f28 \uc2 \f2  could pull out of negotiations to inject $1 billion as part of a debt restructuring plan. The ringgit traded at a low of 4.3030--which represents a 2.2% depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  versus the U.S. dollar as compared to its Tuesday close--but has since recovered to 4.2500. The recovery of the ringgit makes its losses, currently at 0.9%, more in line with that of the similarly embattled Indonesia rupiah, which is down 0.4% today. The two emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have deep-set domestic economic and political issues--sentiment for which cannot be turned around by, for example, China's interest rate and reserve requirement ratio cuts late Tuesday. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0434 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suspected to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market today as the rupiah continues sliding to fresh 17-year lows on regional markets' muted reaction to China's latest round of monetary stimulus. Three traders in Jakarta say they spotted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  selling $100 million so far when the pair was quoted at IDR14,100. "This doesn't seem to be much compared with the daily amount of dollars they sold in the market in the past few days of around $300 million on average per day because some banks also started to sell dollars this morning," a trader says. She adds some banks sold dollars likely on behalf of exporters who need the rupiah to pay taxes or salaries. Bank Indonesia deputy governor Perry Warjiyo said Tuesday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market lately. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has repeatedly said the rupiah has been undervalued. Yet, traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not expected to try to reverse the USD/IDR movement as such a move will deplete its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, which could further hurt the rupiah. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0416 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's export outlook appears more buoyant, particularly after the latest report shows the country's automotive shipments made a recovery in July. The country's overseas shipments of vehicles, which make up more than 8% of total exports, rose 11.52% on-year in July after two consecutive months of contraction due to a change on a pick-up truck model. The recovery in auto shipments is in line with \f28 \uc2 \f2 OCBC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  's and \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  's views, which forecast Thailand's July exports should have contracted 2.55% and 3.8% from last year, respectively, compared with the 7.87% year-over-year contraction in June. "Weak baht will help increase export "volume"," says Tim Leelahaphan of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Maybank Kim Eng Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in an email. Thailand's official trade report for July is due out 0330 GMT Thursday.(nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0413 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities advance in the morning session, but some say the weakness of the rally belies a lack of conviction after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) fifth interest rate cut since November. "As I look at the screen--a few greens, a few reds," writes Lim Say Boon, chief investment officer at DBS Bank . "In short, the market was not impressed." China's high savings rate may mean that any rate cut just means consumers save more to offset lower yields on deposits, having a "counterproductive" effect on economic growth, he says. "You can't stimulate consumption via interest rate cuts," he says. "At least, not in Asia anyway." The Hang Seng Index is up 0.2% at 21,443.80, while H-shares outperform the 0.8% gain in the Shanghai Composite. The Hang Seng Index is up 0.2% at 21,443.80, while H-shares outperform the 0.8% gain in the Shanghai Composite. The China Enterprises Index gains 1.3% to 9,638.14. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; Twitter @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0402 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The ANZ-Roy Morgan Vietnam Consumer Confidence index fell 4.9 points to 133.7 in August, after the authorities resorted to unexpected dong devaluation in response to a weaker Chinese yuan. "The surprise devaluation of the Chinese yuan earlier in the month may have also triggered concerns about a loss of Vietnamese competitiveness," said ANZ economist Glenn Maguire. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vietnam \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week devalued the dong against the U.S. dollar by 1% and widened the dong's trading band to 3% from 2%. "With the Vietnamese economy remaining sure and steady, we note that households are very sensitive to economic news, but expect confidence to stabilize eventually, in line with a resilient economy," he said. (trong-khanh.vu@wsj.com; Twitter: @TrongKhanhVu) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0400 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has cut the reserve-requirement ration for the fifth time since November. Analysts and economists believe it was in response to the panic roiling China's markets, which have shed over 40% of their value since peaking in mid-June. There's another reason, though, for the cut. Government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the yuan, and capital outflows, have wiped out an estimated 600 billion yuan ($93 billion) from the domestic banking system, according to Macquarie's estimates. In light of this, the RRR cut was "needed...to offset this impact," according to economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Larry Hu\f28 \uc2 \f2 . He adds that the cut will free up roughly 700 billion yuan in the banking system. The move is "modestly positive" for the market and economy, but could quell foreign investors' fears of a hard landing. Macquarie expects one more RRR cut in Q4.(anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0355 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Economists are pondering whether to upgrade their GDP growth forecasts for Australia's June quarter, after construction work data published Wednesday was much stronger than many expected. The result was buoyed by a sharp 35% increase in engineering works in Western Australia, even as residential building work fell by 3%. "This means little of itself, but it would be interesting if tomorrow's capital expenditure data were also unusually strong," says Chris Caton, chief economist at BT Financial Group , adding that in this case GDP growth numbers for the second quarter--due on Sept.2--could also be a positive surprise. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0349 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The latest policy action to lower both interest rates and the reserve requirement ratio appears to suggest policy focus is shifting to help the real economy, rather than the stock market, which is a positive development, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  . The house expects more monetary easing is needed to lower funding costs, which has stayed stubbornly high, in order to improve earnings and support equities. The bank notes the China Enterprises Index (H-share index), which track Hong Kong listed Chinese companies, is trading at 5.7 times forward price to earning, or just 4% and 16% higher than the 2011 and 2008 market bottoms, respectively. "As we believe that downward earnings estimates revisions should also be fairly limited, we think now is a good entry point for long-term China investors in terms of H-share prices, although short-term volatilities may continue," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  . The H-share index is up 0.2% at 9,526.26. (yvonne.lee@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 26 Aug 2015 01:23 ET \par Drop in Japan Shares Likely to Prompt Bank of -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0337 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's latest cuts in interest rate and banks' reserve requirement ratio, as well as the partial removal of the deposit rate ceiling, aren't enough to save the stumbling economy, says Citi Research. "The overall pace of policy easing came later than our expectation, and may not be enough to stabilize the economy and thus calm down equity market volatility," the bank notes. It notes Chinese authorities are aiming to stabilize the equity market, the yuan exchange rate, and the economy. However, the rate cut may lead to narrower interest spread between China and the U.S. and thus more fund outflows. "We believe the key to win the triple battle will rely on growth stabilization via a combination of policy easing and meaningful reforms in the real economy, which is more effective if done in a more forward-looking manner," it says. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com, Twitter: @joannechiuhk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 26, 2015 01:23 ET (05:23 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150826eb8q000fa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
