\par\par \b \uc2 Rupiah Falls Again as China Stocks Ignore Easing Measures -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 188 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2015\par 04:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia's rupiah fell again Wednesday, setting another 17-year low - the seventh in a row - as markets reacted in muted fashion to China's latest round of monetary stimulus. USD/IDR hit an intraday high of 14,100, defying hopes that risk appetite might have returned on China's rate cuts announced late Tuesday. China cut its benchmark 1-year interest rates and also slashed the Reserve Requirement Ratio for banks - a constraint on banks' ability to lend. The rupiah has been falling on global economic malaise, triggered by China's slowing economy and its equity bubble popping. Domestically, Indonesia's growth has been lackluster as public spending gets crimped by administrative woes.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but these efforts are unsustainable in the long run, while more fundamental measures such as raising interest rates to prevent capital outflows - which would weaken the rupiah - are unfavorable in this economic environment. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: MNG\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 26-08-15 0213GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150826eb8q00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
