\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  caught in risk asset bloodbath; ringgit, rupiah slide\b0\par\par\uc2 916 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2015\par 08:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Switzerland has opened criminal proceedings linked to 1MDB\par     * Rupiah falls with Jakarta shares down 5 pct\par     * Taiwan dollar, baht hit 6-year lows\par     * Singapore dollar, Philippine peso at 5-year trough\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Aug 24 (Reuters) - Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell\par to their lowest in years on Monday as a rout in Chinese stocks\par accelerated a flight from risky assets on intensifying fears of\par slowing global growth.\par     The Malaysian ringgit hit a fresh pre-peg 17-year\par trough after Switzerland opened criminal proceedings on troubled\par state fund \f28 \uc2 \f2 1Malaysia Development Berhad\f28 \uc2 \f2  (1MDB), adding to the\par country's political woes. \par     Indonesia's rupiah slid to its weakest since the\par Asian financial crisis 17 years ago as falling commodity prices\par clouded its exports and economic prospects.\par     The Taiwan dollar and the Thai baht hit\par six-year lows. The Singapore dollar and the Philippine\par peso also skidded to their weakest since June 2010.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities in Asian countries such as\par South Korea and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses, traders said.\par     Chinese shares tumbled 9 percent, driving regional stocks to\par 3-year troughs. Most government bond prices in the region fell.\par     The cost of insuring against default on sovereign debt rose\par across the board. Credit default swaps rose 10-20 basis points\par (bps), with Thailand, Indonesia and Malaysia seeing the biggest\par rise.\par     Signs of deepening slowdown in China and weak growth in the\par United States and Europe further dented the outlook for the\par global economy, hurting global equities and commodity prices.\par     "Investors need to reduce risk with these sharp moves and it\par doesn't finish in one day," said Sean Yokota, head of Asia\par strategy for Scandinavian bank SEB in Singapore.\par     "People are either looking at the U.S. or China policy\par reaction to change course. Looking at China to deliver a bazooka\par is a mistake and only a dovish Fed can change the direction\par short term," Yokota said.\par     A 50-basis-point cut in China's bank reserve requirement\par ratio (RRR) is not enough to change the current market sell-off,\par he added. Investors had expected \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lower \par RRR during the weekend.\par     With growing risk aversion, the dollar fell against a\par basket of six major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  including the yen. That\par reflected doubts that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will be able to\par raise interest rates next month, traders said.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit fell as much as 1.8 percent to 4.2590 per\par dollar, its weakest since Aug. 11, 1998.\par     Malaysia pegged the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 3.8000 in September 1998 and\par maintained it there until 2005.\par     Switzerland's Office of the Attorney General has opened\par criminal proceedings relating to 1MDB, a spokesman of the Swiss\par authority said on Friday.\par     Kuala Lumpur stocks lost 1.8 percent, while the\par 10-year government bond yield rose to 4.386 percent,\par its highest since July 2009.\par     The ringgit is the worst-performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far\par this year, dragged down by deteriorating fundamentals and the\par fallout from a widening scandal involving the state fund 1MDB.\par     Allegations of graft and financial mismanagement at 1MDB,\par whose advisory board is chaired by Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Najib Razak\f28 \uc2 \f2 ,\par and a slide in oil and commodities prices have acted to push the\par ringgit to 17-year lows. Malaysia is a major supplier of natural\par liquefied gas and palm oil.\par     Malaysia's international reserves fell to $94.5 billion as\par of Aug. 14 from $96.7 billion as of July 31, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data\par showed on Friday. The drop was smaller than a $3.8 billion drop\par in the second half of July. \par     \par     RUPIAH\par     The rupiah lost as much as 0.8 percent to 14,045 per dollar,\par its weakest since July 1998 as foreign investors unloaded the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Indonesian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell in non-deliverable forwards\par markets as managers of foreign reserves sold it,\par traders said. Jakarta shares plunged 5 percent.\par     The rupiah pared some of its earlier losses as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted defending the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's governor said the authority will not take\par part in global competitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation and it is always\par in the market to stabilise the rupiah. \par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0525 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       121.02         121.96       +0.78\par   Sing dlr        1.4121         1.4079       -0.30\par   Taiwan dlr      32.910         32.876       -0.10\par   Korean won     1198.00        1195.00       -0.25\par   Baht             35.77          35.65       -0.34\par   Peso             46.81          46.50       -0.66\par   Rupiah        14020.00       13940.00       -0.57\par   Rupee            66.50          65.83       -1.01\par   Ringgit         4.2240         4.1815       -1.01\par   Yuan            6.3968         6.3887       -0.13\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       121.02         119.66       -1.13\par   Sing dlr        1.4121         1.3260       -6.10\par   Taiwan dlr      32.910         31.718       -3.62\par   Korean won     1198.00        1099.30       -8.24\par   Baht             35.77          32.90       -8.02\par   Peso             46.81          44.72       -4.46\par   Rupiah        14020.00       12380.00      -11.70\par   Rupee            66.50          63.03       -5.21\par   Ringgit         4.2240         3.4965      -17.22\par   Yuan            6.3968         6.2040       -3.01\par  \par \par  (Additional reporting by Umesh Desai in HONG KONG and IFR\par Markets' Catherine Tan; Editing by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jacqueline Wong\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-24T07:33:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150824eb8o006e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
