\par\par \b \uc2 TEXT-Indonesia's c.bank holds rate at 7.50 percent\b0\par\par\uc2 1,237 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2015\par 11:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Aug 18 (Reuters) - \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  on Tuesday held its key policy rate at 7.50 percent, a level it has maintained since February.\par \par \uc2 Following is the text of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement:\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank Indonesia (BI) \uc2 Board of Governors decided on 18th August 2015 to hold the BI Rate at 7.50 percent, while maintaining the Deposit Facility rate at 5.50 percent and the Lending Facility rate at 8.00 percent.\par \par \uc2 The decision is consonant with efforts to control inflation within the target corridor of 4 plus minus 1 percent in 2015 and 2016.\par \par \uc2 In the short term, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  is focused on efforts to stabilize the rupiah amid uncertainty in the global economy, by optimizing monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the rupiah and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  constantly strengthens its monetary and macroprudential policy mix in order to ensure macroeconomic stability, particularly exchange rate and financial system stability to bolster economic sustainability.\par \par \uc2 In addition, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  nurtures sound policy coordination with the Government to expedite pro-growth fiscal stimuli and continues various key structural policies to improve the economic outlook of Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 Global growth remained lower than previously projected amidst widespread uncertainty disrupting global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The global slowdown stemmed from weaker-than-expected growth in the United States coupled with economic moderation in China.\par \par \uc2 Despite the optimistic outlook relayed at the FOMC in July 2015, the U.S. economy is not expected to attain earlier projections for 2015 due to relatively sluggish Q1 and Q2 actuals combined with weak non-residential investment.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, uncertainty regarding the expected FFR hike in the United States endured.\par \par \uc2 Conversely, the economy of Europe was projected to rally on strong domestic demand as unemployment fell.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, distress in Greece also dissipated after bailout conditions were finally agreed. In contrast, economic moderation persisted in China due to the ongoing stock market crisis, with the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  devaluing the yuan to prop up exports and moving towards a market-driven exchange rate, which also exacerbated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in China's trading partners, including Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the torpid global economy perpetuated sliding international commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, uncertainty over the anticipated FFR hike in the United States and China's exchange rate policy were symptomatic of intense risks lingering on global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 On the home front, domestic growth slowed in the second quarter but is projected to rebound in the second semester. Q2/2015 growth was recorded at 4.67 percent (yoy), down from 4.72 percent (yoy) last quarter.\par \par \uc2 The government was less inclined to consume and invest due to slower-than-expected absorption of government spending, including infrastructure project realisation, congruous with the reorganisation at several government ministries/agencies, which led to weaker domestic growth.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the wait-and-see attitude of private investors further compounded the slump in construction investment.\par \par \uc2 Externally, limited export growth was reported in line with the protracted global recovery and soft commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 Regionally, domestic economic moderation was most pronounced on the islands of Sumatra and Kalimantan, with a number of oil and gas rich provinces experiencing negative growth, including Riau, East Kalimantan and Aceh.\par \par \uc2 A smaller current account deficit was indicative of sounder performance. The current account deficit was recorded at $4.5 billion (2.1 percent of GDP) in Q2/2015, shrinking from $9.6 billion (4.3 percent of GDP) in the same period a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Performance was buoyed by a stronger non-oil and gas trade balance due to a dramatic decline in non-oil and gas imports as domestic demand ebbed.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, despite a contraction in non-oil and gas exports (-5.3 percent, yoy), real non-oil and gas shipments rallied as export volume expanded 7.7 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's trade surplus improved to $1.33 billion in July 2015, while the capital and financial account also continued to record a surplus despite ubiquitous uncertainty blighting global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Notwithstanding, the capital and financial account surplus was smaller than that recorded in the same period last year due primarily to declining portfolio investment and a deficit of other investments.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, the position of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve assets at the end of July 2015 was $107.6 billion, equivalent to 7.0 months of imports or 6.8 months of imports and servicing public external debt, which is well above the international adequacy standard of three months.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah depreciated primarily on the back of external sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah fell by an average of 2.47 percent (qtq) to a level of Rp13,131 per U.S. dollar in Q2/2015.\par \par \uc2 Pressures on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  escalated due to investor anticipation of the proposed FFR hike in the United States along with quantitative easing implemented by \uc2 the ECB\uc2  and the ongoing fiscal negotiations in Greece.\par \par \uc2 Domestically, demand surged for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to service debt and disburse seasonal dividend payments during the second quarter of 2015.\par \par \uc2 Pressures were offset, however, by positive sentiment stemming from \uc2 S&P\uc2 's affirmation to raise Indonesia's outlook rating from stable to positive together with a growing trade surplus.\par \par \uc2 The latest developments have shown that, in line with the market reaction to yuan devaluation in China, nearly all global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the rupiah, experienced depreciatory pressures (overshoot).\par \par \uc2 Consequently, the rupiah overshot, which left the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervalued.\par \par \uc2 Addressing such conditions, \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has and will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market in order to stabilise the rupiah in accordance with its fundamental value, thereby supporting macroeconomic and financial system stability.\par \par \uc2 Inflation over the Eid-ul-Fitr period in 2015 was successfully controlled at a rate below the historical average for the past four years.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, core and volatile food inflation were also maintained at low levels.\par \par \uc2 CPI inflation was recorded at 0.93 percent (mtm) in July 2015 or 7.26 percent (yoy) on an annualised basis.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, headline inflation for 2015 through to July was low at just 1.9 percent (ytd). In addition, core inflation was recorded at just 0.34 percent (mtm) or 4.86 percent (yoy), which is low in comparison to its historical trend in line with anchored inflation expectations and domestic economic moderation.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, volatile food inflation was slightly higher than its historical average but controlled at 2.13 percent (mtm) or 8.97 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 Central and local government efforts to stabilise prices helped control pressures on volatile foods.\par \par \uc2 Based on inflation through to July 2015, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  perceives the inflation target of 4 plus minus 1 percent as attainable through policy coordination to control inflation centrally and locally.\par \par \uc2 Financial system stability remained solid, underpinned by a resilient banking system and relatively stable financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The banking industry was again resilient, with credit, liquidity and market risks well mitigated.\par \par \uc2 The Capital Adequacy Ratio (CAR) remained well above the statutory minimum of 8 percent at 20.1 percent in June 2015.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, non-performing loans (NPL) were low and stable at 2.6 percent (gross) or 1.4 percent (net).\par \par \uc2 In terms of the intermediation function, credit growth was stable at 10.4 percent (yoy), while deposits grew 12.7 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 Credit growth is forecasted to accelerate in upcoming periods as the economy improves and \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  loosens its macroprudential policy stance. (Reporting by Gayatri Suroyo; Editing by \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-21T09:36:39.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150818eb8i00c9r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
