\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Firm dollar, weak China factory survey hurt Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2015\par 07:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Won falls on weak exports, PMI; mkt wary of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Rupiah down on bonds, NDFs; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Q1 GDP eyed\par     * Philippine peso lowest in more than a month\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, May 4 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par fell on Monday hurt by a rebound in the dollar ahead of key U.S.\par jobs data and as China's factory activity suffered their fastest\par drop in a year in April.\par     More evidence of a slowdown in the world's second-largest\par economy will hit Asian exporters as China is a key market for\par commodities as well as parts, for re-exports.\par     South Korea's won skidded on disappointing\par economic data. The Indonesian rupiah and the Philippine\par peso slid to their weakest levels in more than a\par month.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 /Markit Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) for China\par fell to 48.9 in April, the lowest level since April 2014, as new\par orders shrank. \par     The survey reinforced expectations for fresh policy stimulus\par from Beijing, which supported regional shares.\par     Still, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders and analysts focused more on the\par dollar's strength with caution mounting ahead of April U.S.\par non-farm payrolls data due on Friday. U.S. employers probably\par added 213,000 jobs last month, compared with 126,000 in March, a\par Reuters poll showed. \par     Expectations of U.S. interest rate hikes later this year\par stayed alive as two top U.S. central bankers said on May 1 that\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  could well raise interest rates as soon as\par June so long as economic data strengthens. \par     "Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will mildly weaken in May as the dollar is\par expected to strengthen on the back of continued positive U.S.\par economic data in the second quarter," said Saktiandi Supaat,\par head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research for Maybank in Singapore.\par     "Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have not priced in possibilities of the\par Fed's rate hike in September."\par     Most regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose in April as dismal U.S.\par economic data in the first quarter caused investors to reduce\par their views that the Fed may hurry to increase borrowing costs.\par     \par     WON\par     The won slid as the economic outlook raised concerns over\par possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities to\par stem strength in the second-best performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so\par far this year.\par     South Korea's manufacturing activity and new export orders\par both contracted for a second month in April, a private sector\par survey showed earlier. \par     Exports in April accelerated their decline for a fourth\par straight month to the worst in two years on lower oil prices and\par weak global demand. \par     Despite such grim data, its finance minister said\par policymakers would probably wait until the end of June to assess\par whether more stimulus measures were needed, causing foreign\par investors to sell treasury bond futures. \par     Local financial markets will be closed on Tuesday for the\par Children's Day holiday.\par     \par     RUPIAH\par     The rupiah lost 0.5 percent to 13,025 per dollar, its\par weakest since April 1.\par     The Indonesian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened in non-deliverable forwards\par , while long-term government bond prices slid.\par     Spot rupiah pared some of its earlier losses as Jakarta\par shares jumped more than 1 percent and as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market to support the\par worst-performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year, traders said.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakness came as Indonesia's economic growth\par in the first quarter is seen having slowed to below 5 percent as\par domestic and export demand softened and global commodity prices\par tumbled, a Reuters poll showed. \par     The growth data will be released on Tuesday.\par     Foreign investors were net sellers in the local stock market\par in the previous seven consecutive sessions, unloading a combined\par net 8.3 trillion rupiah ($638.2 million) during the period,\par Thomson Reuters data showed.\par     \par     PHILIPPINE PESO\par     The peso fell as much as 0.4 percent to 44.71 per dollar,\par its weakest since March 31.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened in most non-deliverable forwards\par  after government bond prices fell in the previous\par session.\par     The 10-year yield on Thursday rose to 4.313\par percent, its highest since Jan. 8. Local financial markets were\par shut last Friday for Labour Day.\par     The peso pared some of its losses on expectations of inflows\par from overseas Filipino workers.\par     The Philippine peso is likely to find support from\par increasing demand linked to remittances, especially as a new\par academic year starts from June, traders said.\par     "Given the seasonality, I am more inclined to expect that\par the peso will continue to trade within the wide 44-45 range\par through May," said a senior Philippine bank trader in Manila.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0510 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  *Japan yen       120.07         120.18       +0.09\par   Sing dlr        1.3319         1.3305       -0.11\par   Taiwan dlr      30.720         30.752       +0.10\par   Korean won     1079.90        1072.40       -0.69\par  *Baht             32.94          32.94       +0.00\par   Peso             44.63          44.52       -0.25\par   Rupiah        13005.00       12960.00       -0.35\par  *Rupee            63.42          63.42       +0.00\par  *Ringgit         3.5600         3.5600       +0.00\par   Yuan            6.2083         6.2028       -0.09\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       120.07         119.66       -0.35\par   Sing dlr        1.3319         1.3260       -0.44\par   Taiwan dlr      30.720         31.718       +3.25\par   Korean won     1079.90        1099.30       +1.80\par   Baht             32.94          32.90       -0.12\par   Peso             44.63          44.72       +0.20\par   Rupiah        13005.00       12380.00       -4.81\par   Rupee            63.42          63.03       -0.61\par   Ringgit         3.5600         3.4965       -1.78\par   Yuan            6.2083         6.2040       -0.07\par   * Financial markets in India, Japan, Malaysia and Thailand are\par closed for holidays.\par \par  (Edit