\par\par \b \uc2 TEXT-Indonesia c.bank hold rates at 7.50 percent\b0\par\par\uc2 1,211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 10:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, March 17 (Reuters) - \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  on Tuesday kept its benchmark interest rate at 7.50 percent.\par \par \uc2 Following is the text of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy statement, with editing by Reuters:\par \par \uc2 The Board of Governors of \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  on March 17, 2015 decided to keep the BI Rate at 7.50 percent, with interest rates of 5.50 percent and Deposit Facility Lending Facility at the level of 8.00 percent. The decision is in line with efforts to achieve the inflation target of 4\u177\'00\'B11 percent in 2015 and 2016, as well as directing the current account deficit to a more healthy level in the range of 2.5-3 percent of GDP in the medium term.\par \par \uc2 Accordingly, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  also strengthens measures to stabilize the rupiah. Policy mix remains focused on maintaining macroeconomic stability amid growing uncertainty in the global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 In this context, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  is committed to strengthen the monetary and macroprudential policy mix, and improve coordination with the Government in controlling inflation and the current account deficit, and accelerate structural reforms.\par \par \uc2 In this regard, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  supports the further steps taken by the Government to carry out structural reforms in order to strengthen the balance of payments.\par \par \uc2 The global economic recovery is continuing, mainly supported by the more-solid U.S. economy. The U.S. economic recovery is supported by the increased consumption due to lower oil prices and the improving labour conditions. The improved U.S. consumption is followed by increasing production indicators.\par \par \uc2 The solid U.S. economy reinforces the direction of monetary policy towards normalization, although the implementation is still uncertain. This encourages the strengthening of the U.S. dollar against almost all world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and increasing uncertainty in the global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, quantitative easing measures from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in addition to offsetting some of the effects of the Fed's policy on the movement of global capital flows to emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the Chinese economy is expected to continue to slow as its investment declined. Correspondingly, global commodity prices, including oil prices, are expected to remain at a low level, although there is an increase compared to the lowest level in the month of January 2015.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's economic growth is expected to start improving and overall in the year will be higher than in 2014. Economic growth in the first quarter of 2015 is expected to increase compared to the previous quarter, mainly supported by private consumption increases with controlled inflation. Government consumption is also improved in line with the increase in government spending.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, exports are expected to still contract, although starting to improve, due to lower commodity prices and weak global demand. On the other hand, investment growth is expected to remain limited in the first quarter of 2015, but will increase in the following quarters with the increase in government capital expenditures.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, economic growth in 2015 is expected to be in the range of 5.4 to 5.8 percent, supported mainly by increased growth of investment with the realization of infrastructure projects and improvement in the investment climate, in addition to consumption that remains strong and gradually improving exports.\par \par \uc2 The trade balance in February 2015 recorded another surplus, mainly driven by non-oil surplus. In February 2015, Indonesia's trade balance recorded a surplus of $0.74 billion, which is relatively stable compared to the previous surplus. The achievement is mainly supported by the non-oil balance surplus of $0.57 billion.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, oil and gas balance in February 2015 also recorded a surplus, after the previous month recorded a deficit. \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  believes the trade surplus in January-February 2015 is in accordance with the current account deficit forecast for the first quarter of 2015, which is seen much lower than in the fourth quarter of 2014.\par \par \uc2 From the financial account, foreign capital inflows are expected to remain strong, influenced by how the domestic economic outlook is getting better.\par \par \uc2 Through February 2015, foreign portfolio inflows into Indonesian financial markets has reached $4.3 billion. With these positive developments, Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in February 2015 increased to $115.5 billion, equivalent to 7.0 months of imports or 6.8 months of imports and foreign debt payments, above the international standard of adequacy of approximately 3 months import.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah against the U.S. dollar weakness is noted, mainly driven by the continued strengthening of the U.S. dollar against all major world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The weakening of the Euro was in line with the quantitative easing pursued by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  further, and it increased the pressure weakening emerging markets' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 In February 2015, the average rupiah weakened 1.38 percent (mtm) to 12.757 per dollar level. In point-to-point, the rupiah depreciated 1.99 percent and closed at 12.925 per U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to increase stabilization measures for the rupiah, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the purchase of government securities in the secondary market. Going forward, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will remain consistent to maintain the stability of the exchange rate corresponding to fundamental conditions.\par \par \uc2 Declining inflationary pressures support the prospect of achieving the inflation target in 2015 which is 4.0\u177\'00\'B11 percent. The Consumer Price Index (CPI) was back in a deflation of 0.36 percent (mtm) and inflation of 6.29 percent (yoy), mainly due to deflation in the volatile components of food and administered prices. Meanwhile, core inflation declined from last month by 0.61 percent (mtm) to 0.34 percent (mtm) or 4.96 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 The decline is supported by the decline in global commodity prices, moderate domestic demand and maintained inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 Going forward, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will continue to examine the various factors that influence the risk of inflation, among others, the development of global oil prices and the possibility of adjustment in other administered prices.\par \par \uc2 The stability of the financial system remains solid, supported by the resilience of the banking system, and the relatively subdued performance of the financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The resilience of the banking industry remains strong with credit risk, liquidity and market maintained, as well as strong capital backing. In January 2015, the capital adequacy ratio (CAR) increased to 20.84 percent, well above the minimum requirement of 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, non-performing loans (NPL) remained low and stable at around 2.0 percent. From the intermediation, credit growth was recorded at 11.5 percent (yoy), relatively stable from the previous month of 11.6 percent (yoy) and is expected to improve in the next months. Meanwhile, growth of deposits in January 2015 stood at 14.2 percent, an increase from the previous month at 12.3 percent (yoy).\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the performance of capital markets also improved, as reflected in an upward trend in the stock index.\par \par \uc2 In the future, in line with the increase in economic growth, deposits and loans growth are expected to accelerate respectively to 15-17 percent and 14-16 percent in 2015. (Reporting by Nilufar Rizki, Adriana Nina Kusuma, and Gayatri Suroyo; Editing by Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-20T09:58:16.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150317eb3h00akl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
