\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  Likely Sold up to $100M to Stem USD/IDR Rise -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2015\par 06:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0522 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  was suspected to have sold dollars between $50 million and $100 million after the USD/IDR rose to 12,830 from 12,715 late Wednesday. Bank Indonesia spokesman Peter Jacob declined to confirm if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but said "\uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is always in the market and will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to prevent the rupiah from falling too sharply." Mr. Jacob added the pair's gain is due to the USD's strength across the board. Dealers said the USD's strength prompted foreigners to take profit on the government's rupiah bonds, sending the 10-year yield up by 20 basis point to 7.4%. Foreigners in January bought around $4 billion of the government's rupiah bond, government data shows. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DMF \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0516 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The market has let PCCW (0008.HK) know that it prefers yield over growth, as shares of the Hong Kong telecom operator fell after the company declared flat dividend growth. PCCW distributed only 72% of dividends it received from HKT (6823.HK) to its shareholders for 2014, a drop from 79% in the previous year. PCCW's 63% stake in HKT, which generates nearly all Ebitda for its parent, and the associated yield is the "primary reasons" for investors to own the stock, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . The company said it is actively looking for growth opportunities globally for its media and technology solutions businesses -- a move that could put its current yield at risk. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  and \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  have both cut their dividend estimates for the stock. PCCW is down 4.7% to HK$5.05 Thursday. (jacky.wong@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @jackycwong) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DAH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0506 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Australian dollar fell to a six-day low of 0.9647 against its Canadian counterpart Thursday, stalling near the 55-day moving average, as an intraday rebound in oil prices (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude was last up 62 cents at $49.46/bbl on Globex) boosted demand for the loonie, while worse-than-expected Australia January jobs data hit sentiment surrounding the Aussie. The AUD/CAD daily price chart is displaying a mixed technical picture with the MACD indicator bearish but the slow stochastic measure neutral. Further downside in the AUD/CAD cross may encounter support at 0.9582 (Feb. 3 reaction low). Spot AUD/CAD is at 0.9671. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0459 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's surprising 5.8% on-year economic growth for the October-to-December quarter will likely prove short-lived as lower oil prices will be a major headwind for the economy this year, Capital Economics says. The economy grew 5.6% on-year during the third quarter. "Looking ahead, the economy faces a couple of challenges. While most economies in emerging Asia will benefit from the fall in oil prices, Malaysia is a key exception given its status as a big net energy exporter," Krystal Tan, an Asia economist, says in a note. "With energy-related revenues set to take a hit, the government will have to further cut back on its spending to prevent its fiscal deficit from ballooning." Capital Economics continue to expect Malaysia's GDP growth to slow at around 5.0% this year, which is broadly in line with its 10-year average and consensus expectations.(saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DMF \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0456 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's expected decline in exports will eventually outweigh its concerns over growing household debt. This could pave the way for a rate cut as early as April, says Nomura economist Kwon Young-sun. "This is because, given that exports remain the main growth engine for Korea, weaker exports should deteriorate corporate performance, which is negative for household income and job markets," Kwon says. Softer exports usually call for \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  to ease its monetary policy while growing debt can justify a policy tightening to put a cap on credit. Nomura maintains that the BOK will lower its base rate twice in April and July to 1.50% to spur growth. The BOK is widely expected to stand pat on rates at its upcoming February 17 meeting. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0452 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Singapore dollar is lower at 88.24 against the yen Thursday versus its late Wednesday level of 88.47 as Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index fell to a four-session low of 3,414.30, spurring fund outflows. The ST Index was recently down 0.8% at 3,416.89. In contrast, Japan's Nikkei Average was recently up 1.91% at 17,989. However, the SGD/JPY daily price chart is still positive-biased as the MACD and slow stochastic indicators are bullish; the five-day moving average is above the 15-day moving average and rising; the rate-of-change momentum indicator is advancing inside positive territory. Further downside in the SGD/JPY cross may encounter support at the middle 20-day Bollinger Band (now at 87.78). (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0450 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Last Thursday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \f28 \uc2 \f2 issued guidelines on when it will require Indian banks to maintain a countercyclical capital buffer, an additional layer of loss-absorbing capital on top of banks' increased minimum capital requirements under Basel III. The RBI guidelines also detail how large the CCCB must be in India under different circumstances, and state that banks will be subject to restrictions on discretionary distributions such as dividend payments, share buybacks and staff bonuses if they do not meet the CCCB requirements. The guidelines are credit positive for Indian banks because they make clear that banks will be required to hold the additional capital amid periods of rapid credit growth. The RBI said it will require banks to hold the full 2.5% buffer when the gap between the credit to GDP ratio and the long-term trend exceeds 15 percentage points. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will implement a CCCB of less than 2.5% when the gap is between three and 15 percentage points, with the size of the required CCCB increasing on a graduated scale. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0445 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Citi sees downsize risk for gold price ahead of possible rate hikes in the U.S. Emerging concerns over Europe "enabled gold to disengage from an inverse relationship with the U.S. dollar strength that had been prevalent since late 2012," it says. Yet the house notes better economic growth prospect in the U.S. has shifted expectation of rates hike to as early as June from September/fourth quarter in 2015. It forecasts gold price to decline by 2% to US$1,240/ounce in 2015 amid a stronger U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and re-affirms its forecasts of an average gold forecast of $1,250/ounce in the first quarter, falling to an average of $1,230/ounce in the second quarter. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @joannechiuhk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0441 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] While unemployment spiked in January in Australia, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not likely to rush headlong into back-to-back rate cuts, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tom Kennedy\f28 \uc2 \f2 , economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Sydney office. He's tipping policymakers will hold fire until May when they will have a better idea about the state of the job market. Data have been volatile for sometime, Kennedy says. "Remember that we have been on a journey to a 6.4% jobless rate already in this cycle, a rise that subsequently was revised away," Kennedy says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0438 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The rebalancing of Australia's economy in the wake of a decade-long mining investment boom is going badly. New South Wales, the services engine of the economy Down Under, recorded a big jump in unemployment to 6.3%, its highest since mid-2009, or when the financial services hub was reeling from the global financial crisis and banks were laying off lots of staff. Job markets in Victoria and Queensland are also soft. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0417 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] CIMB keeps its 'Add' rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Croesus Retail Trust\f28 \uc2 \f2  (S6NU.SG) with an unchanged target at S$1.10 after the real estate investment trust reports a 3% rise in its fiscal second quarter distribution per unit to 2.08 Singapore cents. Croesus reported on Wednesday that profit after tax in the second quarter grew 26% and gross revenue jumped 52%. CIMB says tenant churn at one of its properties will cause slightly higher vacant property units for some time. "We expect the revenue growth momentum to be muted in the third quarter and pick up in the following quarter. Apart from the continued income growth, inorganic drivers such as acquisitions will continue to act as catalysts [for Croesus]," CIMB says. Croesus is currently down 0.5% at S$0.955 while the benchmark FTSE Straits Times Index is down 0.7%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0416 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The door has again opened on \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ASX.AU) losing its monopoly on clearing cash equities in Australia. Two years after the government placed a moratorium on competition and deferred making a decision, Assistant Treasurer Josh Frydenberg says the country's financial regulators have been asked to review the situation. ASX chief executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Elmer Funke Kupper\f28 \uc2 \f2  unsurprisingly argues for a continuation of the current model, which he says would provide market and investment certainty. He notes rival markets in Canada, the U.S. and elsewhere operate only one equities clearing house. He adds Chi-X Australia, which runs a competing trading platform, has access to \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  clearing free of charge. \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2 's return on equity on the clearing business is about 12%, which \f28 \uc2 \f2 Funke Kupper\f28 \uc2 \f2  says is only slightly ahead of the company's cost of capital. So who would benefit from clearing competition? \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2 's boss says it would be high frequency traders. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @RobbMStewart) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 12, 2015 00:22 ET (05:22 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150212eb2c000th\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\