\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  Selling Dollars to Shore up Rupiah: Traders -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2014\par 03:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  is offering dollars at 12,900 rupiah having sold an estimated $200 million to slow the rupiah's fall, dealers say. The USD/IDR is now at 12,900, its new 16-year high, versus 12,700 late Monday as emerging market assets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are under attack on expectation of U.S. rate hike. "A sell-off in global emerging market funds may be contributing to the contagion and general sell-off, including on Indonesia," says \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  economist Hak Bin Chua. \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly said its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are not aimed at defending the rupiah at certain level but to reduce volatility. (I-made.sentana@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Hong Kong equities fall as \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's China flash manufacturing purchasing managers' index for December slides to a seven-month low of 49.5 compared to 50.0 in November, contracting for the first time since May. A reading below 50 indicates contraction. "The manufacturing slowdown continues in December and points to a weak ending for 2014," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "The rising disinflationary pressures, which fundamentally reflect weak demand, warrant further monetary easing in the coming months." The Hang Seng Index falls slightly after the release, down 0.9% to 22,828.98, while H-shares slump; the Hang Seng China Enterprises Index is down 1.1% at 11,092.36. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0211 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/TWD Intraday: the upside prevails. Technically, the pair remains supported by a ST bullish trend line, and continues to post rebounds on an intraday basis. The upward momentum is still strong as the RSI stays firmly above its neutrality area at 50%, without showing any reversal signals. In these perspectives, above 31.17, we suggest LONG positions with targets @ 31.4 & 31.44 in extension. Below 31.17, look for further downside with 31.09 & 30.98 as targets. Trading Central is a commentary service specialising in technical analysis. For more information about TRADING Central please call by phone: London office (+44) 207 429 3980, Paris office (+33) 1 5528 8040 or email: sales@tradingcentral.com. (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0208 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Further weakness in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 /Markit flash Purchasing Managers' Index, which fell to 49.5 in December from 50.0 in November, suggests that smaller Chinese companies continue to face funding problems despite a significant increase in new bank loans in November, said CIMB economist Fan Zhang. This transmission problem dovetails with conversations his firm has had with bank managers. A reading above 50 indicates expansion with a figure below that signals contraction. "The funding conditions with smaller companies are not as expected after the interest rate cut," Zhang said. "This clearly suggests they have to do more easing and more fiscal support." (mark.magnier@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @MarkMagnier) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0151 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY is "on the verge of entering a full-fledged adjustment phase," due to the risk-aversion mood caused by the oil price tumble, says Osamu Takashima, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Global Markets Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a morning note. "The possibility of (the USD/JPY) uptick we have been assuming over the short-term will disappear" if the pair breaks below points on two technical charts simultaneously, Takashima says. The USD/JPY (now at 117.90) is already below the 118.12 base line on an Ichimoku chart, but its relative strength index (now at 53%) is still above the 50% line on a chart tracking market momentum. The pair could drop quickly if it falls below both of these lines. "Depending on the circumstances, the downside support could be around 114," Takashima says. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0146 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  saw no grounds to change the course of monetary policy, minutes of its last meeting showed, even as markets priced in a greater chance of further rate cuts next year. Minutes from the Dec. 2 meeting says "members considered that the most prudent course was likely to be a period of stability in inflation rates." It added that the current stance of monetary policy "continued to be appropriate for fostering sustainable growth in demand and inflation outcomes consistent with the target." The RBA notes that while subdued labor-market conditions were likely to persist, the inflation outlook remained within the 2%-3% target range and a buoyant housing market would likely support household consumption. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0139 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CNH Intraday: bullish bias above 6.185. The pair remains unchanged this morning, and a support base at 6.185 has formed and has allowed for a temporary stabilisation. As long as 6.185 is not broken, the pair is more likely to challenge the nearest key resistance at 6.205 at first, if breakout, look for further advance to 6.217. To sum up, suggest LONG positions above 6.185 with targets @ 6.205 & 6.217 in extension. Below 6.185 look for further downside with 6.17 & 6.148 as targets. China's holdings of US Treasuries fell by $13.6 billion month-over-month to a 20-month low of $1.25 trillion in October, according to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Treasury Department\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Trading Central is a commentary service specialising in technical analysis. For more information about TRADING Central please call by phone: London office (+44) 207 429 3980, Paris office (+33) 1 5528 8040 or email: sales@tradingcentral.com. (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0135 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Nikkei is down 1.8% at 16802.24 in early trading, hit by the dollar's continued weakness (USD/JPY now 117.84), Wall Street's overnight selloff, and fears over Russia's economy, as the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively hiked the interest rate to defend its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against capital flight. "Oil prices continue to slide, and that is now the chief worry to Russia, which is essentially an oil exporting economy," says Yoshihiro Okumura, general manager at Chibagin Asset Management. "The creeping fear is that Russia may default, reminding investors of the prior Greek fiscal panic, and require a bailout. Beyond that, a 'domino effect' of worsening fiscal conditions at other oil-exporting nations may take hold. Oil prices look far from settled at the mid-$50 level, so more pain may yet be in store." \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan Tobacco\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2914.TO), which sports a significant exposure to Russia's market, is down 1.7% a Y3,391. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0131 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Tocom RSS3 rubber futures are lower in slow trade as the yen is stronger versus the dollar Monday. A Tokyo-based analyst says Tocom rubber may face a bumpy ride this week as prices are influenced by external factors including macroeconomic cues from China, the largest importer and consumer of natural rubber. Benchmark May rubber trades Y1.0 lower at Y202.9/kg. (huileng.tan@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @huileng_tan) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0129 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan shares are up 0.2% at 9003.72, as investors are turning optimistic about the outlook of a planned capital gains tax on major stock investors, says a local trader. "Many investors pin hopes that the legislature will likely remove or revise the planned tax on Wednesday so major market players will return to the market," the trader says. But he says low trading volume will likely cap the market's rising momentum. Tech heavyweights are mixed; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (2330.TW) is down 0.7% at NT$135.00, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hon Hai Precision\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2317.TW) falls 0.3% to NT$87.40 but HTC (2498.TW) is up 1.4% at NT$147.50. Cathay Financial (2882.TW) gains 0.4% at NT$45.95 but Fubon Financial (2881.TW) is down 0.5% at NT$48.95. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0125 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Crude-oil futures are headed south again in early Asian trade Tuesday, pushing both Nymex West Texas Intermediate and Brent crude to fresh lows. Oil prices have now lost 47-48% since June. On tap for markets today is the China manufacturing data that could affect market sentiment. "Crude oil markets were down, as fear continued to drive bearish sentiment," ANZ says. It says the oil market is still focused on comments from the UAE energy minister that \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  would refrain from curbing output if prices fell as low as $40/bbl. Nymex WTI is down 45 cents at $55.46/bbl, Brent crude is down 55 cents at $60.51/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0125 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The New Zealand outlook is "rock solid," says ANZ senior economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mark Smith\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Despite a low inflation and commodity-price induced hit to the projected tax take "an improving trend is evident to the fiscal outlook, which will keep the ratings agencies on message," he adds. He says ANZ is not overly concerned "with the modest deterioration in the fiscal accounts" as public finances are moving in the right direction. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 15, 2014 21:17 ET (02:17 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141216eacg000bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
