\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Shows Fatigue As India Continues to Captivate: Blame Inflation -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2014\par 08:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Foreigners continue to pile into the Indian debt market although they have become more cautious towards Indonesia's government bonds, data provided by Nomura Securities show.\par \par \uc2 In November, India saw a strong inflow of 120 billion rupee into its debt markets. "Inflows in the quota allocated for investment by central banks and sovereign wealth funds in government bonds has remained strong (INR21bn vs INR30bn in October) and a further INR20bn in investment in December has pushed the u tilization for this quota to 91%," according to analysts Vivek Rajpal and Prashant Pande. Foreigners also showed interest in corporate debt, increasing their holdings by 119 billion rupee in November, up from INR113 billion in October.\par \par \uc2 Interest in Indonesia, however, started to show fatigue. After a record inflow of 21 trillion Indonesian rupiah into the local debt market in November, "there have already been outflows in December," wrote the Nomura analysts.\par \par \uc2 Inflationary expectation is the culprit. When a foreign investor buys into local debt, she is exposed to inflation as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks. While analysts expect Indonesia's inflation to hit 6.4% this year and 6.95% next, well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 3-5% target, India's consumer price inflation slowed sharply in November to 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah fell to a 16-year low today as local companies bought the U.S. dollar before the year-end. The \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  report:\par \par \uc2 Analysts said that the rupiah was also under pressure because Indonesian companies were buying dollars at the year-end to make scheduled repayments on greenback loans.\par \par \uc2 "The impact on the bond and equity markets and the real economy will be negative," said Fauzi Ichsan, an economist at \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has two choices: to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and shrink its reserves or to hike the FASBI rate [the overnight deposit rate]."\par \par \uc2 Mr Ichsan warned that if \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  did not take action to support the rupiah, there was a risk of a downward spiral.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesia Jakarta Composite Index slumped 1.2%. By comparison, India's S&P BSE Sensex Index was only 0.1% lower.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 Bank of Indonesia\uc2  kept its benchmark rates on hold but was ambiguous towards its future monetary policies. The rupiah fell 1.3% against the dollar while the rupee dropped 0.7%. The \uc2 iShares MSCI Indonesia ETF\uc2  ( EIDO) dropped 3.9%. The \uc2 WisdomTree India Earnings Fund\uc2  ( EPI) retreated 6.5%, mostly on equity losses. The iShares MSCI India ETF ( INDA) fell 6.5%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 15, 2014 02:26 ET (07:26 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141215eacf000go\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
