\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Down Vs USD But May Outperform Peers -\uc2 HSBC\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2014\par 06:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0528 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] India's rupee falls to 61.54 from its Wednesday close of 61.28 versus the U.S. dollar due to a slight change in tone detected in the U.S. Federal Reserve's monetary policy statement released overnight. The greenback has risen across the board but by varying degrees on a more upbeat assessment of the U.S. job market by the \uc2 Fed\uc2 --which prompted traders to price in the possibility of a sooner interest rate rise. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says in a research piece Thursday that the rupee may decline versus the U.S. dollar but may rise versus its peers due to several positive factors. Narrowing current account and budget deficits, together with controlled inflation and the rise of foreign investment inflows ought to benefit India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . India's second-quarter FDI of $8.2 billion was its biggest quarterly inflow since 2011. USD/INR is now 61.45 from its Wednesday close of 61.28. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The NZX-50 closes up 0.3% at 5370.18 led by a rise in Freightways (FRE.NZ) after the company released sharply higher 1Q earnings and said it expected FY15 earnings to be higher on year. Freightways ended the day 6.8% to NZ$5.50. This also supported \uc2 Mainfreight\uc2  (MFT.NZ), which ended the day 3.5% higher at NZ$15.90. Xero (XRO.NZ) remains under pressure on concerns about the company's U.S. growth prospects. Xero ended down 1.9% at NZ$15.85. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;\uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @lucy_craymer) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0425 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese smartphone maker Xiaomi continues to eat into \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Apple\f28 \uc2 \f2 's global market share, moving into the No. 3 position in the third quarter as it enters the list of top five vendors for the first time, according to research firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Data Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , or IDC. Xiaomi had 5.3% of smartphone shipments in the third quarter, behind \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 23.8% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Apple\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 12%. IDC says Xiaomi's growth has been driven by its focus on China and adjacent markets but it remains to be seen whether the company can replicate its success at home overseas. (Gillian.wong@wsj.com @gillianwong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0412 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's official manufacturing purchasing managers' index likely held steady at 51.1 in October, according to the median forecast of eight economists surveyed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Although healthy export demand continues to support the manufacturing sector, cooling domestic demand remains a drag," says Julian Evan-Pritchard of Capital Economics. A preliminary reading of the competing \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  manufacturing PMI, released last week, rose to 50.4 from September's (final) 50.2. The official PMI is due out at 0100 GMT on Saturday, followed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's final reading at 0145 on Monday. (richard.silk@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0406 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sells 5.31 trillion yen of three-month Treasury discount bills with the lowest price of 100.0005, yielding -0.0018%; The MOF said it was the first ever for the highest yield to be negative at the auction. The average yield was also -0.0041%, marking the second consecutive negative rate following the previous auction last week. The Bank of Japan Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday that negative yields in the short-term debt market reflected the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's aggressive asset buying and that they were not "a major problem." Lead December futures price was steady despite a weakening of the yen and higher stock prices. It was down only 0.01 at 146.52. (tatsuo.ito@wsj.comJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0328 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Bank Indonesia Governor Agus Martowardojo hints the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely continue to keep \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market minimal and only will sell dollars if the USD/IDR rises too fast. "We will continue to reduce exchange rate volatility while still giving room for movement that reflects Indonesia's economic fundamentals," he tells business executives at a gathering. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has rarely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets of late despite the fact that the rupiah has occasionally seen selling pressure. "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  likely wants to preserve its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to maintain market confidence that it has enough ammunition needed if massive capital outflows take place," a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer says. (I-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0318 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Kenanga is keeping its Neutral call on the Malaysian plantations sector despite positive palm-oil forecasts from high profile analysts--\f28 \uc2 \f2 James Fry\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomas Mielke\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Dorab Mistry at an industry event in Kuala Lumpur this week--who are predicting average CPO prices of MRY2,400/ton, 9% higher than current spot price at MYR2,195/ton. Kenanga analyst Alan Lim says even with Mr. Mistry's most bullish forecast of MYR2,500/ton by March 2015, the outlook for planters is neutral, as current share prices of the companies have already priced in recovery in CPO prices due to a lower inventory estimate by end-2015 and a decline in 4Q 2014 production. (huileng.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0318 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Nikkei is up 0.5% at 15636.60 midday, helped by the stronger dollar (now 108.97 yen) following the U.S. Fed's somewhat hawkish stance on monetary policy. Solid earnings results from bellwether companies are also helping to boost market confidence. "Buying has been mainly reflexive on the back of the stronger dollar, but there has not been an 'event trade' per se," says an equity trading director at a European brokerage. He notes that foreign hedge funds have increased their net long positions substantially after squaring some three trillion yen in short trades though the third week of October. Keyence (6861.TO) is up 5.6% at 50,270 yen after its first-half operating profits beat forecasts. Capcom (9697.TO) is up 4.9% at 1,704 yen after its second-quarter operating profits came in broadly on a par with consensus. Makita (6586.TO) is up 2.9% after raising its first half operating profit guidance. \f28 \uc2 \f2 SoftBank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (9984.TO) is up 1.9% at 7,650 yen following a slew of positive brokerage reports. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0255 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Philippine's peso is weaker Thursday due to broad U.S. dollar strength, but could avoid depreciating further in the near term if USD/PHP ends the day below 44.85. Currently at 44.85 versus its Wednesday close of 44.72, the dollar is perched at the entrance to the daily Bollinger uptrend channel after a bout of overnight strength triggered by an unexpectedly upbeat assessment of the U.S. job market by the U.S. Federal Reserve. A Thursday close above 44.85 could give USD/PHP the impetus to keep climbing toward the round-figure trading barrier at 45.00. Analysts now think the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  could raise interest rates earlier than the consensus forecast for the second-half of 2015. Later today, third-quarter U.S. gross domestic product data could motivate USD-bulls if the number comes in stronger than expectations for a 3.1% rise. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0248 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese investment in foreign stocks remains strong with net purchases of Y197 billion in the week Oct. 19-25, stretching the streak of net buying to eight weeks in a row, according to Japan's MOF. The amount of Japanese net foreign stock buying over the past four weeks, including the latest data, comes to nearly Y1 trillion, accelerating the pace from the net purchases of more than Y2 trillion seen in the whole of the July-Sept quarter. Aggressive foreign stock buying by Japanese was one of the two drivers for the rally in USD/JPY in August and September along with expectations of rate rises in the U.S., says Tohru Sasaki, head of Japan Rates & Fixed Research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Now the FOMC last night may heighten the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rate hike expectations further in coming days, whether Japanese will continue their foreign stock investments should be important to push up the USD/JPY further," he says. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0243 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The yuan falls after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guides it weaker via daily fixing to track the dollar's broad gains overnight. The yuan is at 6.1138 to a dollar, compared with 6.1112 at Wednesday's close. The central parity is set at 6.1457 versus 6.1405 at Wednesday. "But the yuan's weakness is capped as the large Chinese banks are ready to sell the dollar at any time," says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. The yuan hit fresh seven-month highs in the past four sessions in a row. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY non-deliverable forwards are up at 6.2370/6.2385 from 6.2320/6.2360 late Wednesday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0220 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/IDR rose to 12,180--implying a weaker rupiah versus the U.S. dollar--and stocks fell 0.5% as the market reacted to a surprisingly hawkish-sounding U.S. Federal Reserve. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  highlighted strong job growth in its post-FOMC statement, but also said that inflation likely remains muted. The change in its perception of the U.S. job market may signal that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  is stepping toward raising interest rates. This spooked greenback-shorts and sent USD/Asia broadly higher Thursday. USD/IDR could enter the Bollinger uptrend channel on a Thursday closing above 12,220--which may spur more U.S. dollar-buying. USD/IDR is now 12,150 from its last close of 12,075. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 30, 2014 01:28 ET (05:28 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141030eaau000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\p